{"surah": 13, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "الٓمٓرۚ تِلْكَ ءَايَٰتُ ٱلْكِتَٰبِۗ وَٱلَّذِىٓ أُنزِلَ إِلَيْكَ مِن رَّبِّكَ ٱلْحَقُّ وَلَٰكِنَّ أَكْثَرَ ٱلنَّاسِ لَا يُؤْمِنُونَ ", "translation": "Alif, Lām, Meem, Rā.[1] These are the verses of the Book; and what has been revealed to you from your Lord is the truth, but most of the people do not believe.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "الٓمٓر", "translit": "rٓmٓlā", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تِلْكَ", "translit": "َkْlِt", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أُنزِلَ", "translit": "َlِznُaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رَّبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقُّ", "translit": "ُّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِkَٰlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَكْثَرَ", "translit": "َrَhtْkَaʾ", "root": "كثر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "ٱللَّهُ ٱلَّذِى رَفَعَ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ بِغَيْرِ عَمَدٍ تَرَوْنَهَاۖ ثُمَّ ٱسْتَوَىٰ عَلَى ٱلْعَرْشِۖ وَسَخَّرَ ٱلشَّمْسَ وَٱلْقَمَرَۖ كُلٌّ يَجْرِى لِأَجَلٍ مُّسَمًّىۚ يُدَبِّرُ ٱلْأَمْرَ يُفَصِّلُ ٱلْءَايَٰتِ لَعَلَّكُم بِلِقَآءِ رَبِّكُمْ تُوقِنُونَ ", "translation": "It is Allāh who erected the heavens without pillars that you [can] see; then He established Himself above the Throne[1] and made subject[2] the sun and the moon, each running [its course] for a specified term. He arranges [each] matter; He details the signs that you may, of the meeting with your Lord, be certain.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَفَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَfَr", "root": "رفع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِغَيْرِ", "translit": "ِrْyَغِb", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَمَدٍ", "translit": "ٍdَmَʿع", "root": "عمد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تَرَوْنَهَا", "translit": "āَhَnْwَrَt", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱسْتَوَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَwَtْsٱ", "root": "سوي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْعَرْشِ", "translit": "ِhsْrَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عرش"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَسَخَّرَ", "translit": "َrَّhkَsَw", "root": "سخر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلشَّمْسَ", "translit": "َsْmَّhslٱ", "root": "شمس"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَٱلْقَمَرَ", "translit": "َrَmَqْlٱَw", "root": "قمر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "كُلٌّ", "translit": "ٌّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يَجْرِى", "translit": "àِrْjgَy", "root": "جري"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لِأَجَلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَjgَaʾِl", "root": "اجل"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "مُّسَمًّى", "translit": "àًّmَsُّm", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "يُدَبِّرُ", "translit": "ُrِّbَdُy", "root": "دبر"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْرَ", "translit": "َrْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "يُفَصِّلُ", "translit": "ُlِّṣَfُy", "root": "فصل"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ٱلْءَايَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰyāَءْlٱ", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "لَعَلَّكُم", "translit": "mُkَّlَʿعَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "بِلِقَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَqِlِb", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "رَبِّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "تُوقِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِqwُt", "root": "يقن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "وَهُوَ ٱلَّذِى مَدَّ ٱلْأَرْضَ وَجَعَلَ فِيهَا رَوَٰسِىَ وَأَنْهَٰرًاۖ وَمِن كُلِّ ٱلثَّمَرَٰتِ جَعَلَ فِيهَا زَوْجَيْنِ ٱثْنَيْنِۖ يُغْشِى ٱلَّيْلَ ٱلنَّهَارَۚ إِنَّ فِى ذَٰلِكَ لَءَايَٰتٍ لِّقَوْمٍ يَتَفَكَّرُونَ ", "translation": "And it is He who spread the earth and placed therein firmly set mountains and rivers; and from all of the fruits He made therein two mates; He causes the night to cover the day. Indeed in that are signs for a people who give thought.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَدَّ", "translit": "َّdَm", "root": "مدد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَجَعَلَ", "translit": "َlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَوَٰسِىَ", "translit": "َàِsَٰwَr", "root": "رسو"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَأَنْهَٰرًا", "translit": "āًrَٰhْnَaʾَw", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَمِن", "translit": "nِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلثَّمَرَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰrَmَّhtlٱ", "root": "ثمر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "جَعَلَ", "translit": "َlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "زَوْجَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَjgْwَz", "root": "زوج"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱثْنَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَnْhtٱ", "root": "ثني"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يُغْشِى", "translit": "àِhsْغُy", "root": "غشو"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلَّيْلَ", "translit": "َlْyَّlٱ", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلنَّهَارَ", "translit": "َrāَhَّnlٱ", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "لَءَايَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰyāَءَl", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "لِّقَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَqِّl", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "يَتَفَكَّرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَّkَfَtَy", "root": "فكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "وَفِى ٱلْأَرْضِ قِطَعٌ مُّتَجَٰوِرَٰتٌ وَجَنَّٰتٌ مِّنْ أَعْنَٰبٍ وَزَرْعٌ وَنَخِيلٌ صِنْوَانٌ وَغَيْرُ صِنْوَانٍ يُسْقَىٰ بِمَآءٍ وَٰحِدٍ وَنُفَضِّلُ بَعْضَهَا عَلَىٰ بَعْضٍ فِى ٱلْأُكُلِۚ إِنَّ فِى ذَٰلِكَ لَءَايَٰتٍ لِّقَوْمٍ يَعْقِلُونَ ", "translation": "And within the land are neighboring plots and gardens of grapevines and crops and palm trees, [growing] several from a root or otherwise,[1] watered with one water; but We make some of them exceed others in [quality of] fruit. Indeed in that are signs for a people who reason.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَفِى", "translit": "àِfَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قِطَعٌ", "translit": "ٌʿعَṭِq", "root": "قطع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مُّتَجَٰوِرَٰتٌ", "translit": "ٌtَٰrِwَٰjgَtُّm", "root": "جور"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَجَنَّٰتٌ", "translit": "ٌtَّٰnَjgَw", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَعْنَٰبٍ", "translit": "ٍbَٰnْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عنب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَزَرْعٌ", "translit": "ٌʿعْrَzَw", "root": "زرع"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَنَخِيلٌ", "translit": "ٌlyِhkَnَw", "root": "نخل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "صِنْوَانٌ", "translit": "ٌnāَwْnِṣ", "root": "صنو"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَغَيْرُ", "translit": "ُrْyَغَw", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "صِنْوَانٍ", "translit": "ٍnāَwْnِṣ", "root": "صنو"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يُسْقَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَqْsُy", "root": "سقي"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بِمَآءٍ", "translit": "ٍءٓāَmِb", "root": "موه"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَٰحِدٍ", "translit": "ٍdِḥَٰw", "root": "وحد"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَنُفَضِّلُ", "translit": "ُlِّḍَfُnَw", "root": "فضل"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بَعْضَهَا", "translit": "āَhَḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "بَعْضٍ", "translit": "ٍḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱلْأُكُلِ", "translit": "ِlُkُaʾْlٱ", "root": "اكل"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "لَءَايَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰyāَءَl", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "لِّقَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَqِّl", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "يَعْقِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِqْʿعَy", "root": "عقل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "وَإِن تَعْجَبْ فَعَجَبٌ قَوْلُهُمْ أَءِذَا كُنَّا تُرَٰبًا أَءِنَّا لَفِى خَلْقٍ جَدِيدٍۗ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ بِرَبِّهِمْۖ وَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ ٱلْأَغْلَٰلُ فِىٓ أَعْنَاقِهِمْۖ وَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ أَصْحَٰبُ ٱلنَّارِۖ هُمْ فِيهَا خَٰلِدُونَ ", "translation": "And if you are astonished,[1] [O Muḥammad] - then astonishing is their saying, \"When we are dust, will we indeed be [brought] into a new creation?\" Those are the ones who have disbelieved in their Lord, and those will have shackles[2] upon their necks, and those are the companions of the Fire; they will abide therein eternally.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَعْجَبْ", "translit": "ْbَjgْʿعَt", "root": "عجب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَعَجَبٌ", "translit": "ٌbَjgَʿعَf", "root": "عجب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قَوْلُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُlْwَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَءِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِءَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تُرَٰبًا", "translit": "āًbَٰrُt", "root": "ترب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَءِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِءَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَفِى", "translit": "àِfَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "خَلْقٍ", "translit": "ٍqْlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "جَدِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِdَjg", "root": "جدد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "بِرَبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَrِb", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْأَغْلَٰلُ", "translit": "ُlَٰlْغَaʾْlٱ", "root": "غلل"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "فِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "أَعْنَاقِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِqāَnْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عنق"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "أَصْحَٰبُ", "translit": "ُbَٰḥْṣَaʾ", "root": "صحب"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ٱلنَّارِ", "translit": "ِrāَّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "خَٰلِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِlَٰhk", "root": "خلد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "وَيَسْتَعْجِلُونَكَ بِٱلسَّيِّئَةِ قَبْلَ ٱلْحَسَنَةِ وَقَدْ خَلَتْ مِن قَبْلِهِمُ ٱلْمَثُلَٰتُۗ وَإِنَّ رَبَّكَ لَذُو مَغْفِرَةٍ لِّلنَّاسِ عَلَىٰ ظُلْمِهِمْۖ وَإِنَّ رَبَّكَ لَشَدِيدُ ٱلْعِقَابِ ", "translation": "They impatiently urge you to bring about evil before good,[1] while there has already occurred before them similar punishments [to what they demand]. And indeed, your Lord is the possessor of forgiveness for the people despite their wrongdoing, and indeed, your Lord is severe in penalty.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَسْتَعْجِلُونَكَ", "translit": "َkَnwُlِjgْʿعَtْsَyَw", "root": "عجل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِٱلسَّيِّئَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَyʾِّyَّslٱِb", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَبْلَ", "translit": "َlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْحَسَنَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَnَsَḥْlٱ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "خَلَتْ", "translit": "ْtَlَhk", "root": "خلو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قَبْلِهِمُ", "translit": "ُmِhِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْمَثُلَٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَٰlُhtَmْlٱ", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "رَبَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَذُو", "translit": "wُhdَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مَغْفِرَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَrِfْغَm", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لِّلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlِّl", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ظُلْمِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِmْlُẓ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "رَبَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "لَشَدِيدُ", "translit": "ُdyِdَhsَl", "root": "شدد"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلْعِقَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَqِʿعْlٱ", "root": "عقب"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "وَيَقُولُ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ لَوْلَآ أُنزِلَ عَلَيْهِ ءَايَةٌ مِّن رَّبِّهِۦٓۗ إِنَّمَآ أَنتَ مُنذِرٌۖ وَلِكُلِّ قَوْمٍ هَادٍ ", "translation": "And those who disbelieved say, \"Why has a sign not been sent down to him from his Lord?\" You are only a warner, and for every people is a guide.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَyَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَوْلَآ", "translit": "ٓāَlْwَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أُنزِلَ", "translit": "َlِznُaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ءَايَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رَّبِّهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِنَّمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مُنذِرٌ", "translit": "ٌrِhdnُm", "root": "نذر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَلِكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِlَw", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "قَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "هَادٍ", "translit": "ٍdāَh", "root": "هدي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "ٱللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ مَا تَحْمِلُ كُلُّ أُنثَىٰ وَمَا تَغِيضُ ٱلْأَرْحَامُ وَمَا تَزْدَادُۖ وَكُلُّ شَىْءٍ عِندَهُۥ بِمِقْدَارٍ ", "translation": "Allāh knows what every female carries[1] and what the wombs lose [prematurely] or exceed.[2] And everything with Him is by due measure.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَحْمِلُ", "translit": "ُlِmْḥَt", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أُنثَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhtnُaʾ", "root": "انث"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَغِيضُ", "translit": "ُḍyِغَt", "root": "غيض"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْحَامُ", "translit": "ُmāَḥْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَزْدَادُ", "translit": "ُdāَdْzَt", "root": "زيد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَكُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُkَw", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عِندَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "بِمِقْدَارٍ", "translit": "ٍrāَdْqِmِb", "root": "قدر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "عَٰلِمُ ٱلْغَيْبِ وَٱلشَّهَٰدَةِ ٱلْكَبِيرُ ٱلْمُتَعَالِ ", "translation": "[He is] Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, the Grand, the Exalted.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "عَٰلِمُ", "translit": "ُmِlَٰʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْغَيْبِ", "translit": "ِbْyَغْlٱ", "root": "غيب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَٱلشَّهَٰدَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَdَٰhَّhslٱَw", "root": "شهد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْكَبِيرُ", "translit": "ُryِbَkْlٱ", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْمُتَعَالِ", "translit": "ِlāَʿعَtُmْlٱ", "root": "علو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "سَوَآءٌ مِّنكُم مَّنْ أَسَرَّ ٱلْقَوْلَ وَمَن جَهَرَ بِهِۦ وَمَنْ هُوَ مُسْتَخْفٍۭ بِٱلَّيْلِ وَسَارِبٌۢ بِٱلنَّهَارِ ", "translation": "It is the same [to Him] concerning you whether one conceals [his] speech or publicizes it and whether one is hidden by night or conspicuous [among others] by day.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سَوَآءٌ", "translit": "ٌءٓāَwَs", "root": "سوي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّنكُم", "translit": "mُknِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَّنْ", "translit": "ْnَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَسَرَّ", "translit": "َّrَsَaʾ", "root": "سرر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْلَ", "translit": "َlْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "جَهَرَ", "translit": "َrَhَjg", "root": "جهر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مُسْتَخْفٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭfْhkَtْsُm", "root": "خفي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِٱلَّيْلِ", "translit": "ِlْyَّlٱِb", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَسَارِبٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢbِrāَsَw", "root": "سرب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بِٱلنَّهَارِ", "translit": "ِrāَhَّnlٱِb", "root": "نهر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "لَهُۥ مُعَقِّبَٰتٌ مِّنۢ بَيْنِ يَدَيْهِ وَمِنْ خَلْفِهِۦ يَحْفَظُونَهُۥ مِنْ أَمْرِ ٱللَّهِۗ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَا يُغَيِّرُ مَا بِقَوْمٍ حَتَّىٰ يُغَيِّرُوا۟ مَا بِأَنفُسِهِمْۗ وَإِذَآ أَرَادَ ٱللَّهُ بِقَوْمٍ سُوٓءًا فَلَا مَرَدَّ لَهُۥۚ وَمَا لَهُم مِّن دُونِهِۦ مِن وَالٍ ", "translation": "For him [i.e., each one] are successive [angels][1] before and behind him who protect him by the decree of Allāh.[2] Indeed, Allāh will not change the condition of a people until they change what is in themselves. And when Allāh intends for a people ill,[3] there is no repelling it. And there is not for them besides Him any patron.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مُعَقِّبَٰتٌ", "translit": "ٌtَٰbِّqَʿعُm", "root": "عقب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِّنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَدَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَdَy", "root": "يدي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمِنْ", "translit": "ْnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "خَلْفِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِfْlَhk", "root": "خلف"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَحْفَظُونَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَnwُẓَfْḥَy", "root": "حفظ"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَمْرِ", "translit": "ِrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يُغَيِّرُ", "translit": "ُrِّyَغُy", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بِقَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَqِb", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "يُغَيِّرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrِّyَغُy", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "بِأَنفُسِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِsُfnَaʾِb", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "وَإِذَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "أَرَادَ", "translit": "َdāَrَaʾ", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "بِقَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَqِb", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "سُوٓءًا", "translit": "āًءٓwُs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "مَرَدَّ", "translit": "َّdَrَm", "root": "ردد"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "لَهُم", "translit": "mُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "دُونِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "وَالٍ", "translit": "ٍlāَw", "root": "ولي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "هُوَ ٱلَّذِى يُرِيكُمُ ٱلْبَرْقَ خَوْفًا وَطَمَعًا وَيُنشِئُ ٱلسَّحَابَ ٱلثِّقَالَ ", "translation": "It is He who shows you lightning, [causing] fear and aspiration, and generates the heavy clouds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُرِيكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkyِrُy", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْبَرْقَ", "translit": "َqْrَbْlٱ", "root": "برق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "خَوْفًا", "translit": "āًfْwَhk", "root": "خوف"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَطَمَعًا", "translit": "āًʿعَmَṭَw", "root": "طمع"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَيُنشِئُ", "translit": "ُyʾِhsnُyَw", "root": "نشا"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلسَّحَابَ", "translit": "َbāَḥَّslٱ", "root": "سحب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلثِّقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqِّhtlٱ", "root": "ثقل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "وَيُسَبِّحُ ٱلرَّعْدُ بِحَمْدِهِۦ وَٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ مِنْ خِيفَتِهِۦ وَيُرْسِلُ ٱلصَّوَٰعِقَ فَيُصِيبُ بِهَا مَن يَشَآءُ وَهُمْ يُجَٰدِلُونَ فِى ٱللَّهِ وَهُوَ شَدِيدُ ٱلْمِحَالِ ", "translation": "And the thunder exalts [Allāh] with praise of Him - and the angels [as well] from fear of Him - and He sends thunderbolts and strikes therewith whom He wills while they dispute about Allāh; and He is severe in assault.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيُسَبِّحُ", "translit": "ُḥِّbَsُyَw", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلرَّعْدُ", "translit": "ُdْʿعَّrlٱ", "root": "رعد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِحَمْدِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdْmَḥِb", "root": "حمد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَkِyʾَٰٓlَmْlٱَw", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "خِيفَتِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِtَfyِhk", "root": "خوف"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَيُرْسِلُ", "translit": "ُlِsْrُyَw", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلصَّوَٰعِقَ", "translit": "َqِʿعَٰwَّṣlٱ", "root": "صعق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَيُصِيبُ", "translit": "ُbyِṣُyَf", "root": "صوب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِهَا", "translit": "āَhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يُجَٰدِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِdَٰjgُy", "root": "جدل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "شَدِيدُ", "translit": "ُdyِdَhs", "root": "شدد"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱلْمِحَالِ", "translit": "ِlāَḥِmْlٱ", "root": "محل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "لَهُۥ دَعْوَةُ ٱلْحَقِّۖ وَٱلَّذِينَ يَدْعُونَ مِن دُونِهِۦ لَا يَسْتَجِيبُونَ لَهُم بِشَىْءٍ إِلَّا كَبَٰسِطِ كَفَّيْهِ إِلَى ٱلْمَآءِ لِيَبْلُغَ فَاهُ وَمَا هُوَ بِبَٰلِغِهِۦۚ وَمَا دُعَآءُ ٱلْكَٰفِرِينَ إِلَّا فِى ضَلَٰلٍ ", "translation": "To Him [alone] is the supplication of truth. And those they call upon besides Him do not respond to them with a thing, except as one who stretches his hands toward water [from afar, calling it] to reach his mouth, but it will not reach it [thus].[1] And the supplication of the disbelievers is not but in error [i.e., futility].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "دَعْوَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَwْʿعَd", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَدْعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعْdَy", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "دُونِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَسْتَجِيبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbyِjgَtْsَy", "root": "جوب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَهُم", "translit": "mُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بِشَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhsِb", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "كَبَٰسِطِ", "translit": "ِṭِsَٰbَk", "root": "بسط"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَفَّيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَّfَk", "root": "كفف"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmْlٱ", "root": "موه"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لِيَبْلُغَ", "translit": "َغُlْbَyِl", "root": "بلغ"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "فَاهُ", "translit": "ُhāَf", "root": "فوه"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "بِبَٰلِغِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِغِlَٰbِb", "root": "بلغ"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "دُعَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَʿعُd", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ٱلْكَٰفِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِfَٰkْlٱ", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "ضَلَٰلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَٰlَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "وَلِلَّهِ يَسْجُدُ مَن فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ طَوْعًا وَكَرْهًا وَظِلَٰلُهُم بِٱلْغُدُوِّ وَٱلْءَاصَالِ۩ ", "translation": "And to Allāh prostrates whoever is within the heavens and the earth, willingly or by compulsion, and their shadows [as well] in the mornings and the afternoons.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِlَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَسْجُدُ", "translit": "ُdُjgْsَy", "root": "سجد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "طَوْعًا", "translit": "āًʿعْwَṭ", "root": "طوع"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَكَرْهًا", "translit": "āًhْrَkَw", "root": "كره"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَظِلَٰلُهُم", "translit": "mُhُlَٰlِẓَw", "root": "ظلل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِٱلْغُدُوِّ", "translit": "ِّwُdُغْlٱِb", "root": "غدو"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَٱلْءَاصَالِ", "translit": "ِlāَṣāَءْlٱَw", "root": "اصل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "قُلْ مَن رَّبُّ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ قُلِ ٱللَّهُۚ قُلْ أَفَٱتَّخَذْتُم مِّن دُونِهِۦٓ أَوْلِيَآءَ لَا يَمْلِكُونَ لِأَنفُسِهِمْ نَفْعًا وَلَا ضَرًّاۚ قُلْ هَلْ يَسْتَوِى ٱلْأَعْمَىٰ وَٱلْبَصِيرُ أَمْ هَلْ تَسْتَوِى ٱلظُّلُمَٰتُ وَٱلنُّورُۗ أَمْ جَعَلُوا۟ لِلَّهِ شُرَكَآءَ خَلَقُوا۟ كَخَلْقِهِۦ فَتَشَٰبَهَ ٱلْخَلْقُ عَلَيْهِمْۚ قُلِ ٱللَّهُ خَٰلِقُ كُلِّ شَىْءٍ وَهُوَ ٱلْوَٰحِدُ ٱلْقَهَّٰرُ ", "translation": "Say, \"Who is Lord of the heavens and earth?\" Say, \"Allāh.\" Say, \"Have you then taken besides Him allies not possessing [even] for themselves any benefit or any harm?\" Say, \"Is the blind equivalent to the seeing? Or is darkness equivalent to light? Or have they attributed to Allāh partners who created like His creation so that the creation [of each] seemed similar to them?\"[1] Say, \"Allāh is the Creator of all things, and He is the One, the Prevailing.\"[2]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَّبُّ", "translit": "ُّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قُلِ", "translit": "ِlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَفَٱتَّخَذْتُم", "translit": "mُtْhdَhkَّtٱَfَaʾ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "دُونِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَوْلِيَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَyِlْwَaʾ", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يَمْلِكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkِlْmَy", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لِأَنفُسِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِsُfnَaʾِl", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "نَفْعًا", "translit": "āًʿعْfَn", "root": "نفع"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ضَرًّا", "translit": "āًّrَḍ", "root": "ضرر"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "هَلْ", "translit": "ْlَh", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "يَسْتَوِى", "translit": "àِwَtْsَy", "root": "سوي"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ٱلْأَعْمَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَmْʿعَaʾْlٱ", "root": "عمي"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "وَٱلْبَصِيرُ", "translit": "ُryِṣَbْlٱَw", "root": "بصر"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "هَلْ", "translit": "ْlَh", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "تَسْتَوِى", "translit": "àِwَtْsَt", "root": "سوي"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "ٱلظُّلُمَٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَٰmُlُّẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "وَٱلنُّورُ", "translit": "ُrwُّnlٱَw", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "جَعَلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "شُرَكَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَkَrُhs", "root": "شرك"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "خَلَقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "كَخَلْقِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِqْlَhkَk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "فَتَشَٰبَهَ", "translit": "َhَbَٰhsَtَf", "root": "شبه"}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "ٱلْخَلْقُ", "translit": "ُqْlَhkْlٱ", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 38, "arabic": "قُلِ", "translit": "ِlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 39, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 40, "arabic": "خَٰلِقُ", "translit": "ُqِlَٰhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 41, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 42, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 43, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 44, "arabic": "ٱلْوَٰحِدُ", "translit": "ُdِḥَٰwْlٱ", "root": "وحد"}, {"id": 45, "arabic": "ٱلْقَهَّٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَّٰhَqْlٱ", "root": "قهر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "أَنزَلَ مِنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ مَآءً فَسَالَتْ أَوْدِيَةٌۢ بِقَدَرِهَا فَٱحْتَمَلَ ٱلسَّيْلُ زَبَدًا رَّابِيًاۚ وَمِمَّا يُوقِدُونَ عَلَيْهِ فِى ٱلنَّارِ ٱبْتِغَآءَ حِلْيَةٍ أَوْ مَتَٰعٍ زَبَدٌ مِّثْلُهُۥۚ كَذَٰلِكَ يَضْرِبُ ٱللَّهُ ٱلْحَقَّ وَٱلْبَٰطِلَۚ فَأَمَّا ٱلزَّبَدُ فَيَذْهَبُ جُفَآءًۖ وَأَمَّا مَا يَنفَعُ ٱلنَّاسَ فَيَمْكُثُ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِۚ كَذَٰلِكَ يَضْرِبُ ٱللَّهُ ٱلْأَمْثَالَ ", "translation": "He sends down from the sky, rain, and valleys flow according to their capacity, and the torrent carries a rising foam. And from that [ore] which they heat in the fire, desiring adornments and utensils, is a foam like it. Thus Allāh presents [the example of] truth and falsehood. As for the foam, it vanishes, [being] cast off; but as for that which benefits the people, it remains on the earth. Thus does Allāh present examples.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَنزَلَ", "translit": "َlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَآءً", "translit": "ًءٓāَm", "root": "موه"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَسَالَتْ", "translit": "ْtَlāَsَf", "root": "سيل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَوْدِيَةٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢẗَyِdْwَaʾ", "root": "ودي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِقَدَرِهَا", "translit": "āَhِrَdَqِb", "root": "قدر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَٱحْتَمَلَ", "translit": "َlَmَtْḥٱَf", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلسَّيْلُ", "translit": "ُlْyَّslٱ", "root": "سيل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "زَبَدًا", "translit": "āًdَbَz", "root": "زبد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "رَّابِيًا", "translit": "āًyِbāَّr", "root": "ربو"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَمِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يُوقِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِqwُy", "root": "وقد"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلنَّارِ", "translit": "ِrāَّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱبْتِغَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَغِtْbٱ", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "حِلْيَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَyْlِḥ", "root": "حلي"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "مَتَٰعٍ", "translit": "ٍʿعَٰtَm", "root": "متع"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "زَبَدٌ", "translit": "ٌdَbَz", "root": "زبد"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "مِّثْلُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُlْhtِّm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "كَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "يَضْرِبُ", "translit": "ُbِrْḍَy", "root": "ضرب"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقَّ", "translit": "َّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "وَٱلْبَٰطِلَ", "translit": "َlِṭَٰbْlٱَw", "root": "بطل"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "فَأَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "ٱلزَّبَدُ", "translit": "ُdَbَّzlٱ", "root": "زبد"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "فَيَذْهَبُ", "translit": "ُbَhْhdَyَf", "root": "ذهب"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "جُفَآءً", "translit": "ًءٓāَfُjg", "root": "جفا"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "وَأَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "يَنفَعُ", "translit": "ُʿعَfnَy", "root": "نفع"}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسَ", "translit": "َsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "فَيَمْكُثُ", "translit": "ُhtُkْmَyَf", "root": "مكث"}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 38, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 39, "arabic": "كَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 40, "arabic": "يَضْرِبُ", "translit": "ُbِrْḍَy", "root": "ضرب"}, {"id": 41, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 42, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْثَالَ", "translit": "َlāَhtْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "مثل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "لِلَّذِينَ ٱسْتَجَابُوا۟ لِرَبِّهِمُ ٱلْحُسْنَىٰۚ وَٱلَّذِينَ لَمْ يَسْتَجِيبُوا۟ لَهُۥ لَوْ أَنَّ لَهُم مَّا فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ جَمِيعًا وَمِثْلَهُۥ مَعَهُۥ لَٱفْتَدَوْا۟ بِهِۦٓۚ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ لَهُمْ سُوٓءُ ٱلْحِسَابِ وَمَأْوَىٰهُمْ جَهَنَّمُۖ وَبِئْسَ ٱلْمِهَادُ ", "translation": "For those who have responded to their Lord is the best [reward], but those who did not respond to Him - if they had all that is in the earth entirely and the like of it with it, they would [attempt to] ransom themselves thereby. Those will have the worst account, and their refuge is Hell, and wretched is the resting place.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لِلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱسْتَجَابُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbāَjgَtْsٱ", "root": "جوب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِرَبِّهِمُ", "translit": "ُmِhِّbَrِl", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَnْsُḥْlٱ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَسْتَجِيبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbyِjgَtْsَy", "root": "جوب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَوْ", "translit": "ْwَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَهُم", "translit": "mُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "جَمِيعًا", "translit": "āًʿعyِmَjg", "root": "جمع"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَمِثْلَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlْhtِmَw", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مَعَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَʿعَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "لَٱفْتَدَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَdَtْfٱَl", "root": "فدي"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "بِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "سُوٓءُ", "translit": "ُءٓwُs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱلْحِسَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَsِḥْlٱ", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "وَمَأْوَىٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhٰàَwْaʾَmَw", "root": "اوي"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمُ", "translit": "ُmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "وَبِئْسَ", "translit": "َsْyʾِbَw", "root": "باس"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "ٱلْمِهَادُ", "translit": "ُdāَhِmْlٱ", "root": "مهد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "أَفَمَن يَعْلَمُ أَنَّمَآ أُنزِلَ إِلَيْكَ مِن رَّبِّكَ ٱلْحَقُّ كَمَنْ هُوَ أَعْمَىٰٓۚ إِنَّمَا يَتَذَكَّرُ أُو۟لُوا۟ ٱلْأَلْبَٰبِ ", "translation": "Then is he who knows that what has been revealed to you from your Lord is the truth like one who is blind? They will only be reminded who are people of understanding -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَمَن", "translit": "nَmَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَّمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أُنزِلَ", "translit": "َlِznُaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَّبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقُّ", "translit": "ُّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَعْمَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يَتَذَكَّرُ", "translit": "ُrَّkَhdَtَy", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أُو۟لُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْأَلْبَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰbْlَaʾْlٱ", "root": "لبب"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 20, "text": "ٱلَّذِينَ يُوفُونَ بِعَهْدِ ٱللَّهِ وَلَا يَنقُضُونَ ٱلْمِيثَٰقَ ", "translation": "Those who fulfill the covenant of Allāh and do not break the contract,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُوفُونَ", "translit": "َnwُfwُy", "root": "وفي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِعَهْدِ", "translit": "ِdْhَʿعِb", "root": "عهد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَنقُضُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḍُqnَy", "root": "نقض"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْمِيثَٰقَ", "translit": "َqَٰhtyِmْlٱ", "root": "وثق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ يَصِلُونَ مَآ أَمَرَ ٱللَّهُ بِهِۦٓ أَن يُوصَلَ وَيَخْشَوْنَ رَبَّهُمْ وَيَخَافُونَ سُوٓءَ ٱلْحِسَابِ ", "translation": "And those who join that which Allāh has ordered to be joined[1] and fear their Lord and are afraid of the evil of [their] account,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَصِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِṣَy", "root": "وصل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَمَرَ", "translit": "َrَmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يُوصَلَ", "translit": "َlَṣwُy", "root": "وصل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَيَخْشَوْنَ", "translit": "َnْwَhsْhkَyَw", "root": "خشي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "رَبَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَيَخَافُونَ", "translit": "َnwُfāَhkَyَw", "root": "خوف"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "سُوٓءَ", "translit": "َءٓwُs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْحِسَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَsِḥْlٱ", "root": "حسب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 22, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ صَبَرُوا۟ ٱبْتِغَآءَ وَجْهِ رَبِّهِمْ وَأَقَامُوا۟ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ وَأَنفَقُوا۟ مِمَّا رَزَقْنَٰهُمْ سِرًّا وَعَلَانِيَةً وَيَدْرَءُونَ بِٱلْحَسَنَةِ ٱلسَّيِّئَةَ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ لَهُمْ عُقْبَى ٱلدَّارِ ", "translation": "And those who are patient, seeking the face [i.e., acceptance] of their Lord, and establish prayer and spend from what We have provided for them secretly and publicly and prevent evil with good - those will have the good consequence of [this] home[1] -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "صَبَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَbَṣ", "root": "صبر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱبْتِغَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَغِtْbٱ", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَجْهِ", "translit": "ِhْjgَw", "root": "وجه"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رَبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَقَامُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmāَqَaʾَw", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَأَنفَقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَfnَaʾَw", "root": "نفق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "رَزَقْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْqَzَr", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "سِرًّا", "translit": "āًّrِs", "root": "سرر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَعَلَانِيَةً", "translit": "ًẗَyِnāَlَʿعَw", "root": "علن"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَيَدْرَءُونَ", "translit": "َnwُءَrْdَyَw", "root": "درا"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَسَنَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَnَsَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلسَّيِّئَةَ", "translit": "َẗَyʾِّyَّslٱ", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "عُقْبَى", "translit": "àَbْqُʿع", "root": "عقب"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱلدَّارِ", "translit": "ِrāَّdlٱ", "root": "دور"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 23, "text": "جَنَّٰتُ عَدْنٍ يَدْخُلُونَهَا وَمَن صَلَحَ مِنْ ءَابَآئِهِمْ وَأَزْوَٰجِهِمْ وَذُرِّيَّٰتِهِمْۖ وَٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ يَدْخُلُونَ عَلَيْهِم مِّن كُلِّ بَابٍ ", "translation": "Gardens of perpetual residence; they will enter them with whoever were righteous among their forefathers, their spouses and their descendants. And the angels will enter upon them from every gate, [saying],", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "جَنَّٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَّٰnَjg", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَدْنٍ", "translit": "ٍnْdَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَدْخُلُونَهَا", "translit": "āَhَnwُlُhkْdَy", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "صَلَحَ", "translit": "َḥَlَṣ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ءَابَآئِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِyʾٓāَbāَء", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَأَزْوَٰجِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِjgَٰwْzَaʾَw", "root": "زوج"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَذُرِّيَّٰتِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِtَّٰyِّrُhdَw", "root": "ذرر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَkِyʾَٰٓlَmْlٱَw", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَدْخُلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlُhkْdَy", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِم", "translit": "mِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "بَابٍ", "translit": "ٍbāَb", "root": "بوب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 24, "text": "سَلَٰمٌ عَلَيْكُم بِمَا صَبَرْتُمْۚ فَنِعْمَ عُقْبَى ٱلدَّارِ ", "translation": "\"Peace [i.e., security] be upon you for what you patiently endured.\" And excellent is the final home.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سَلَٰمٌ", "translit": "ٌmَٰlَs", "root": "سلم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُم", "translit": "mُkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "صَبَرْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْrَbَṣ", "root": "صبر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَنِعْمَ", "translit": "َmْʿعِnَf", "root": "نعم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عُقْبَى", "translit": "àَbْqُʿع", "root": "عقب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلدَّارِ", "translit": "ِrāَّdlٱ", "root": "دور"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 25, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ يَنقُضُونَ عَهْدَ ٱللَّهِ مِنۢ بَعْدِ مِيثَٰقِهِۦ وَيَقْطَعُونَ مَآ أَمَرَ ٱللَّهُ بِهِۦٓ أَن يُوصَلَ وَيُفْسِدُونَ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِۙ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ لَهُمُ ٱللَّعْنَةُ وَلَهُمْ سُوٓءُ ٱلدَّارِ ", "translation": "But those who break the covenant of Allāh after contracting it and sever that which Allāh has ordered to be joined and spread corruption on earth - for them is the curse, and they will have the worst home.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَنقُضُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḍُqnَy", "root": "نقض"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَهْدَ", "translit": "َdْhَʿع", "root": "عهد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بَعْدِ", "translit": "ِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِيثَٰقِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِqَٰhtyِm", "root": "وثق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَيَقْطَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَṭْqَyَw", "root": "قطع"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَمَرَ", "translit": "َrَmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يُوصَلَ", "translit": "َlَṣwُy", "root": "وصل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَيُفْسِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِsْfُyَw", "root": "فسد"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "لَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱللَّعْنَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَnْʿعَّllٱ", "root": "لعن"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "سُوٓءُ", "translit": "ُءٓwُs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱلدَّارِ", "translit": "ِrāَّdlٱ", "root": "دور"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 26, "text": "ٱللَّهُ يَبْسُطُ ٱلرِّزْقَ لِمَن يَشَآءُ وَيَقْدِرُۚ وَفَرِحُوا۟ بِٱلْحَيَوٰةِ ٱلدُّنْيَا وَمَا ٱلْحَيَوٰةُ ٱلدُّنْيَا فِى ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ إِلَّا مَتَٰعٌ ", "translation": "Allāh extends provision for whom He wills and restricts [it]. And they rejoice in the worldly life, while the worldly life is not, compared to the Hereafter, except [brief] enjoyment.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَبْسُطُ", "translit": "ُṭُsْbَy", "root": "بسط"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلرِّزْقَ", "translit": "َqْzِّrlٱ", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِمَن", "translit": "nَmِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَيَقْدِرُ", "translit": "ُrِdْqَyَw", "root": "قدر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَفَرِحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥِrَfَw", "root": "فرح"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَيَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَyَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْحَيَوٰةُ", "translit": "ُẗٰwَyَḥْlٱ", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مَتَٰعٌ", "translit": "ٌʿعَٰtَm", "root": "متع"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "وَيَقُولُ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ لَوْلَآ أُنزِلَ عَلَيْهِ ءَايَةٌ مِّن رَّبِّهِۦۗ قُلْ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يُضِلُّ مَن يَشَآءُ وَيَهْدِىٓ إِلَيْهِ مَنْ أَنَابَ ", "translation": "And those who disbelieved say, \"Why has a sign not been sent down to him from his Lord?\" Say, [O Muḥammad], \"Indeed, Allāh leaves astray whom He wills and guides to Himself whoever turns back [to Him] -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَyَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَوْلَآ", "translit": "ٓāَlْwَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أُنزِلَ", "translit": "َlِznُaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ءَايَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رَّبِّهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يُضِلُّ", "translit": "ُّlِḍُy", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَيَهْدِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِdْhَyَw", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "مَنْ", "translit": "ْnَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "أَنَابَ", "translit": "َbāَnَaʾ", "root": "نوب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَتَطْمَئِنُّ قُلُوبُهُم بِذِكْرِ ٱللَّهِۗ أَلَا بِذِكْرِ ٱللَّهِ تَطْمَئِنُّ ٱلْقُلُوبُ ", "translation": "Those who have believed and whose hearts are assured by the remembrance of Allāh. Unquestionably, by the remembrance of Allāh hearts are assured.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَتَطْمَئِنُّ", "translit": "ُّnِyʾَmْṭَtَw", "root": "طمان"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قُلُوبُهُم", "translit": "mُhُbwُlُq", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِذِكْرِ", "translit": "ِrْkِhdِb", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَلَا", "translit": "āَlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِذِكْرِ", "translit": "ِrْkِhdِb", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تَطْمَئِنُّ", "translit": "ُّnِyʾَmْṭَt", "root": "طمان"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْقُلُوبُ", "translit": "ُbwُlُqْlٱ", "root": "قلب"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ طُوبَىٰ لَهُمْ وَحُسْنُ مَـَٔابٍ ", "translation": "Those who have believed and done righteous deeds - a good state is theirs and a good return.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَʿعَw", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "طُوبَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَbwُṭ", "root": "طيب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَحُسْنُ", "translit": "ُnْsُḥَw", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَـَٔابٍ", "translit": "ٍbāَٔـَm", "root": "اوب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "كَذَٰلِكَ أَرْسَلْنَٰكَ فِىٓ أُمَّةٍ قَدْ خَلَتْ مِن قَبْلِهَآ أُمَمٌ لِّتَتْلُوَا۟ عَلَيْهِمُ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَوْحَيْنَآ إِلَيْكَ وَهُمْ يَكْفُرُونَ بِٱلرَّحْمَٰنِۚ قُلْ هُوَ رَبِّى لَآ إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا هُوَ عَلَيْهِ تَوَكَّلْتُ وَإِلَيْهِ مَتَابِ ", "translation": "Thus have We sent you to a community before which [other] communities have passed on so you might recite to them that which We revealed to you, while they disbelieve in the Most Merciful. Say, \"He is my Lord; there is no deity except Him. Upon Him I rely, and to Him is my return.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَٰكَ", "translit": "َkَٰnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أُمَّةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَّmُaʾ", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "خَلَتْ", "translit": "ْtَlَhk", "root": "خلو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قَبْلِهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أُمَمٌ", "translit": "ٌmَmُaʾ", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِّتَتْلُوَا۟", "translit": "۟āَwُlْtَtِّl", "root": "تلو"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمُ", "translit": "ُmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَوْحَيْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْyَḥْwَaʾ", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يَكْفُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُfْkَy", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بِٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlٱِb", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "رَبِّى", "translit": "àِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "لَآ", "translit": "ٓāَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "إِلَٰهَ", "translit": "َhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "تَوَكَّلْتُ", "translit": "ُtْlَّkَwَt", "root": "وكل"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "وَإِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "مَتَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَtَm", "root": "توب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 31, "text": "وَلَوْ أَنَّ قُرْءَانًا سُيِّرَتْ بِهِ ٱلْجِبَالُ أَوْ قُطِّعَتْ بِهِ ٱلْأَرْضُ أَوْ كُلِّمَ بِهِ ٱلْمَوْتَىٰۗ بَل لِّلَّهِ ٱلْأَمْرُ جَمِيعًاۗ أَفَلَمْ يَا۟يْـَٔسِ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوٓا۟ أَن لَّوْ يَشَآءُ ٱللَّهُ لَهَدَى ٱلنَّاسَ جَمِيعًاۗ وَلَا يَزَالُ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ تُصِيبُهُم بِمَا صَنَعُوا۟ قَارِعَةٌ أَوْ تَحُلُّ قَرِيبًا مِّن دَارِهِمْ حَتَّىٰ يَأْتِىَ وَعْدُ ٱللَّهِۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَا يُخْلِفُ ٱلْمِيعَادَ ", "translation": "And if there was any Qur’ān [i.e., recitation] by which the mountains would be removed or the earth would be broken apart or the dead would be made to speak,[1] [it would be this Qur’ān], but to Allāh belongs the affair entirely. Then have those who believed not accepted that had Allāh willed, He would have guided the people, all of them? And those who disbelieve do not cease to be struck, for what they have done, by calamity - or it will descend near their home - until there comes the promise of Allāh. Indeed, Allāh does not fail in [His] promise.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَوْ", "translit": "ْwَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قُرْءَانًا", "translit": "āًnāَءْrُq", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "سُيِّرَتْ", "translit": "ْtَrِّyُs", "root": "سير"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِهِ", "translit": "ِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْجِبَالُ", "translit": "ُlāَbِjgْlٱ", "root": "جبل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قُطِّعَتْ", "translit": "ْtَʿعِّṭُq", "root": "قطع"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِهِ", "translit": "ِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضُ", "translit": "ُḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كُلِّمَ", "translit": "َmِّlُk", "root": "كلم"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بِهِ", "translit": "ِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْمَوْتَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَtْwَmْlٱ", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "بَل", "translit": "lَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لِّلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِّl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْرُ", "translit": "ُrْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "جَمِيعًا", "translit": "āًʿعyِmَjg", "root": "جمع"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "أَفَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "يَا۟يْـَٔسِ", "translit": "ِsَٔـْy۟āَy", "root": "ياس"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "لَّوْ", "translit": "ْwَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "لَهَدَى", "translit": "àَdَhَl", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسَ", "translit": "َsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "جَمِيعًا", "translit": "āًʿعyِmَjg", "root": "جمع"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "يَزَالُ", "translit": "ُlāَzَy", "root": "زيل"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "تُصِيبُهُم", "translit": "mُhُbyِṣُt", "root": "صوب"}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "صَنَعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعَnَṣ", "root": "صنع"}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "قَارِعَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَʿعِrāَq", "root": "قرع"}, {"id": 38, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 39, "arabic": "تَحُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُḥَt", "root": "حلل"}, {"id": 40, "arabic": "قَرِيبًا", "translit": "āًbyِrَq", "root": "قرب"}, {"id": 41, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 42, "arabic": "دَارِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِrāَd", "root": "دور"}, {"id": 43, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 44, "arabic": "يَأْتِىَ", "translit": "َàِtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 45, "arabic": "وَعْدُ", "translit": "ُdْʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 46, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 47, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 48, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 49, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 50, "arabic": "يُخْلِفُ", "translit": "ُfِlْhkُy", "root": "خلف"}, {"id": 51, "arabic": "ٱلْمِيعَادَ", "translit": "َdāَʿعyِmْlٱ", "root": "وعد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 32, "text": "وَلَقَدِ ٱسْتُهْزِئَ بِرُسُلٍ مِّن قَبْلِكَ فَأَمْلَيْتُ لِلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ ثُمَّ أَخَذْتُهُمْۖ فَكَيْفَ كَانَ عِقَابِ ", "translation": "And already were [other] messengers ridiculed before you, and I extended the time of those who disbelieved; then I seized them, and how [terrible] was My penalty.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدِ", "translit": "ِdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱسْتُهْزِئَ", "translit": "َyʾِzْhُtْsٱ", "root": "هزا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِرُسُلٍ", "translit": "ٍlُsُrِb", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَبْلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَأَمْلَيْتُ", "translit": "ُtْyَlْmَaʾَf", "root": "ملو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لِلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَخَذْتُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُtْhdَhkَaʾ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فَكَيْفَ", "translit": "َfْyَkَf", "root": "كيف"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عِقَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَqِʿع", "root": "عقب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 33, "text": "أَفَمَنْ هُوَ قَآئِمٌ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ نَفْسٍۭ بِمَا كَسَبَتْۗ وَجَعَلُوا۟ لِلَّهِ شُرَكَآءَ قُلْ سَمُّوهُمْۚ أَمْ تُنَبِّـُٔونَهُۥ بِمَا لَا يَعْلَمُ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ أَم بِظَٰهِرٍ مِّنَ ٱلْقَوْلِۗ بَلْ زُيِّنَ لِلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ مَكْرُهُمْ وَصُدُّوا۟ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِۗ وَمَن يُضْلِلِ ٱللَّهُ فَمَا لَهُۥ مِنْ هَادٍ ", "translation": "Then is He who is a maintainer of every soul, [knowing] what it has earned, [like any other]? But to Allāh they have attributed partners. Say, \"Name them. Or do you inform Him of that[1] which He knows not upon the earth or of what is apparent [i.e., alleged] of speech?\"[2] Rather, their [own] plan has been made attractive to those who disbelieve, and they have been averted from the way. And whomever Allāh sends astray - there will be for him no guide.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَآئِمٌ", "translit": "ٌmِyʾٓāَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نَفْسٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭsْfَn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَسَبَتْ", "translit": "ْtَbَsَk", "root": "كسب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَجَعَلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "شُرَكَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَkَrُhs", "root": "شرك"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "سَمُّوهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhwُّmَs", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "تُنَبِّـُٔونَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَnwُٔـِّbَnُt", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "أَم", "translit": "mَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "بِظَٰهِرٍ", "translit": "ٍrِhَٰẓِb", "root": "ظهر"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْلِ", "translit": "ِlْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "زُيِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّyُz", "root": "زين"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "لِلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "مَكْرُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُrْkَm", "root": "مكر"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "وَصُدُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّdُṣَw", "root": "صدد"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "عَنِ", "translit": "ِnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "ٱلسَّبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَّslٱ", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "يُضْلِلِ", "translit": "ِlِlْḍُy", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "فَمَا", "translit": "āَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 38, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 39, "arabic": "هَادٍ", "translit": "ٍdāَh", "root": "هدي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 34, "text": "لَّهُمْ عَذَابٌ فِى ٱلْحَيَوٰةِ ٱلدُّنْيَاۖ وَلَعَذَابُ ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ أَشَقُّۖ وَمَا لَهُم مِّنَ ٱللَّهِ مِن وَاقٍ ", "translation": "For them will be punishment in the life of [this] world, and the punishment of the Hereafter is more severe. And they will not have from Allāh any protector.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَذَابٌ", "translit": "ٌbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْحَيَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَyَḥْlٱ", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَلَعَذَابُ", "translit": "ُbāَhdَʿعَlَw", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَشَقُّ", "translit": "ُّqَhsَaʾ", "root": "شقق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَهُم", "translit": "mُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَاقٍ", "translit": "ٍqāَw", "root": "وقي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 35, "text": "مَّثَلُ ٱلْجَنَّةِ ٱلَّتِى وُعِدَ ٱلْمُتَّقُونَۖ تَجْرِى مِن تَحْتِهَا ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُۖ أُكُلُهَا دَآئِمٌ وَظِلُّهَاۚ تِلْكَ عُقْبَى ٱلَّذِينَ ٱتَّقَوا۟ۖ وَّعُقْبَى ٱلْكَٰفِرِينَ ٱلنَّارُ ", "translation": "The example [i.e., description] of Paradise, which the righteous have been promised, is [that] beneath it rivers flow. Its fruit is lasting, and its shade. That is the consequence for the righteous, and the consequence for the disbelievers is the Fire.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَّثَلُ", "translit": "ُlَhtَّm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْجَنَّةِ", "translit": "ِẗَّnَjgْlٱ", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وُعِدَ", "translit": "َdِʿعُw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْمُتَّقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqَّtُmْlٱ", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَجْرِى", "translit": "àِrْjgَt", "root": "جري"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَحْتِهَا", "translit": "āَhِtْḥَt", "root": "تحت"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَٰhْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أُكُلُهَا", "translit": "āَhُlُkُaʾ", "root": "اكل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "دَآئِمٌ", "translit": "ٌmِyʾٓāَd", "root": "دوم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَظِلُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّlِẓَw", "root": "ظلل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تِلْكَ", "translit": "َkْlِt", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عُقْبَى", "translit": "àَbْqُʿع", "root": "عقب"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱتَّقَوا۟", "translit": "۟āwَqَّtٱ", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَّعُقْبَى", "translit": "àَbْqُʿعَّw", "root": "عقب"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلْكَٰفِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِfَٰkْlٱ", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱلنَّارُ", "translit": "ُrāَّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 36, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ ءَاتَيْنَٰهُمُ ٱلْكِتَٰبَ يَفْرَحُونَ بِمَآ أُنزِلَ إِلَيْكَۖ وَمِنَ ٱلْأَحْزَابِ مَن يُنكِرُ بَعْضَهُۥۚ قُلْ إِنَّمَآ أُمِرْتُ أَنْ أَعْبُدَ ٱللَّهَ وَلَآ أُشْرِكَ بِهِۦٓۚ إِلَيْهِ أَدْعُوا۟ وَإِلَيْهِ مَـَٔابِ ", "translation": "And [the believers among] those to whom We have given the [previous] Scripture rejoice at what has been revealed to you, [O Muḥammad], but among the [opposing] factions are those who deny part of it [i.e., the Qur’ān]. Say, \"I have only been commanded to worship Allāh and not associate [anything] with Him. To Him I invite, and to Him is my return.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَاتَيْنَٰهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَٰnْyَtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبَ", "translit": "َbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَفْرَحُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḥَrْfَy", "root": "فرح"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أُنزِلَ", "translit": "َlِznُaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَحْزَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَzْḥَaʾْlٱ", "root": "حزب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يُنكِرُ", "translit": "ُrِknُy", "root": "نكر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بَعْضَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِنَّمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "أُمِرْتُ", "translit": "ُtْrِmُaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَنْ", "translit": "ْnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أَعْبُدَ", "translit": "َdُbْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَلَآ", "translit": "ٓāَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أُشْرِكَ", "translit": "َkِrْhsُaʾ", "root": "شرك"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "بِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "أَدْعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعْdَaʾ", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "وَإِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "مَـَٔابِ", "translit": "ِbāَٔـَm", "root": "اوب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 37, "text": "وَكَذَٰلِكَ أَنزَلْنَٰهُ حُكْمًا عَرَبِيًّاۚ وَلَئِنِ ٱتَّبَعْتَ أَهْوَآءَهُم بَعْدَمَا جَآءَكَ مِنَ ٱلْعِلْمِ مَا لَكَ مِنَ ٱللَّهِ مِن وَلِىٍّ وَلَا وَاقٍ ", "translation": "And thus We have revealed it as an Arabic legislation.[1] And if you should follow their inclinations after what has come to you of knowledge, you would not have against Allāh any ally or any protector.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَkَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنزَلْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "حُكْمًا", "translit": "āًmْkُḥ", "root": "حكم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَرَبِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِbَrَʿع", "root": "عرب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَلَئِنِ", "translit": "ِnِyʾَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱتَّبَعْتَ", "translit": "َtْʿعَbَّtٱ", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَهْوَآءَهُم", "translit": "mُhَءٓāَwْhَaʾ", "root": "هوي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بَعْدَمَا", "translit": "āَmَdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "جَآءَكَ", "translit": "َkَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْعِلْمِ", "translit": "ِmْlِʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَكَ", "translit": "َkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَلِىٍّ", "translit": "ٍّàِlَw", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَاقٍ", "translit": "ٍqāَw", "root": "وقي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 38, "text": "وَلَقَدْ أَرْسَلْنَا رُسُلًا مِّن قَبْلِكَ وَجَعَلْنَا لَهُمْ أَزْوَٰجًا وَذُرِّيَّةًۚ وَمَا كَانَ لِرَسُولٍ أَن يَأْتِىَ بِـَٔايَةٍ إِلَّا بِإِذْنِ ٱللَّهِۗ لِكُلِّ أَجَلٍ كِتَابٌ ", "translation": "And We have already sent messengers before you and assigned to them wives and descendants. And it was not for a messenger to come with a sign except by permission of Allāh. For every term is a decree.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رُسُلًا", "translit": "āًlُsُr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَبْلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَزْوَٰجًا", "translit": "āًjgَٰwْzَaʾ", "root": "زوج"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَذُرِّيَّةً", "translit": "ًẗَّyِّrُhdَw", "root": "ذرر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لِرَسُولٍ", "translit": "ٍlwُsَrِl", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يَأْتِىَ", "translit": "َàِtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بِإِذْنِ", "translit": "ِnْhdِiʾِb", "root": "اذن"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "لِكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِl", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أَجَلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَjgَaʾ", "root": "اجل"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "كِتَابٌ", "translit": "ٌbāَtِk", "root": "كتب"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 39, "text": "يَمْحُوا۟ ٱللَّهُ مَا يَشَآءُ وَيُثْبِتُۖ وَعِندَهُۥٓ أُمُّ ٱلْكِتَٰبِ ", "translation": "Allāh eliminates what He wills or confirms, and with Him is the Mother of the Book.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَمْحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥْmَy", "root": "محو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَيُثْبِتُ", "translit": "ُtِbْhtُyَw", "root": "ثبت"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَعِندَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَdnِʿعَw", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أُمُّ", "translit": "ُّmُaʾ", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 40, "text": "وَإِن مَّا نُرِيَنَّكَ بَعْضَ ٱلَّذِى نَعِدُهُمْ أَوْ نَتَوَفَّيَنَّكَ فَإِنَّمَا عَلَيْكَ ٱلْبَلَٰغُ وَعَلَيْنَا ٱلْحِسَابُ ", "translation": "And whether We show you part of what We promise them or take you in death, upon you is only the [duty of] notification, and upon Us is the account.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نُرِيَنَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّnَyِrُn", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَعْضَ", "translit": "َḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نَعِدُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُdِʿعَn", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "نَتَوَفَّيَنَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّnَyَّfَwَtَn", "root": "وفي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَإِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْبَلَٰغُ", "translit": "ُغَٰlَbْlٱ", "root": "بلغ"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَعَلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlَʿعَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْحِسَابُ", "translit": "ُbāَsِḥْlٱ", "root": "حسب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 41, "text": "أَوَلَمْ يَرَوْا۟ أَنَّا نَأْتِى ٱلْأَرْضَ نَنقُصُهَا مِنْ أَطْرَافِهَاۚ وَٱللَّهُ يَحْكُمُ لَا مُعَقِّبَ لِحُكْمِهِۦۚ وَهُوَ سَرِيعُ ٱلْحِسَابِ ", "translation": "Have they not seen that We set upon the land, reducing it from its borders?[1] And Allāh decides; there is no adjuster of His decision. And He is swift in account.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَوَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَرَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَrَy", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَّا", "translit": "āَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نَأْتِى", "translit": "àِtْaʾَn", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نَنقُصُهَا", "translit": "āَhُṣُqnَn", "root": "نقص"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَطْرَافِهَا", "translit": "āَhِfāَrْṭَaʾ", "root": "طرف"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَحْكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkْḥَy", "root": "حكم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مُعَقِّبَ", "translit": "َbِّqَʿعُm", "root": "عقب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لِحُكْمِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِmْkُḥِl", "root": "حكم"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "سَرِيعُ", "translit": "ُʿعyِrَs", "root": "سرع"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْحِسَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَsِḥْlٱ", "root": "حسب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 42, "text": "وَقَدْ مَكَرَ ٱلَّذِينَ مِن قَبْلِهِمْ فَلِلَّهِ ٱلْمَكْرُ جَمِيعًاۖ يَعْلَمُ مَا تَكْسِبُ كُلُّ نَفْسٍۗ وَسَيَعْلَمُ ٱلْكُفَّٰرُ لِمَنْ عُقْبَى ٱلدَّارِ ", "translation": "And those before them had plotted, but to Allāh belongs the plan entirely. He knows what every soul earns, and the disbelievers will know for whom is the final home.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَكَرَ", "translit": "َrَkَm", "root": "مكر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَبْلِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَلِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِlَf", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْمَكْرُ", "translit": "ُrْkَmْlٱ", "root": "مكر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "جَمِيعًا", "translit": "āًʿعyِmَjg", "root": "جمع"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَكْسِبُ", "translit": "ُbِsْkَt", "root": "كسب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "نَفْسٍ", "translit": "ٍsْfَn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَسَيَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَyَsَw", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْكُفَّٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَّٰfُkْlٱ", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لِمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "عُقْبَى", "translit": "àَbْqُʿع", "root": "عقب"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلدَّارِ", "translit": "ِrāَّdlٱ", "root": "دور"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 43, "text": "وَيَقُولُ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ لَسْتَ مُرْسَلًاۚ قُلْ كَفَىٰ بِٱللَّهِ شَهِيدًۢا بَيْنِى وَبَيْنَكُمْ وَمَنْ عِندَهُۥ عِلْمُ ٱلْكِتَٰبِ ", "translation": "And those who have disbelieved say, \"You are not a messenger.\" Say, [O Muḥammad], \"Sufficient is Allāh as Witness between me and you, and [the witness of] whoever has knowledge of the Scripture.\"[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَyَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَسْتَ", "translit": "َtْsَl", "root": "ليس"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مُرْسَلًا", "translit": "āًlَsْrُm", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَفَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَfَk", "root": "كفي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "شَهِيدًۢا", "translit": "āًۢdyِhَhs", "root": "شهد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بَيْنِى", "translit": "àِnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَبَيْنَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَnْyَbَw", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عِندَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عِلْمُ", "translit": "ُmْlِʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"4": [{"arabic": "الأشكال والألوان والطعوم والصفات والأرابيح يفضل بعضها على بعض في الأكل تسقى بماء واحد وتخرج من أرض واحدة فإن قلت إن اختلافها باختلاف بذورها وأصولها فمتى كان فى النواة نخلة مطوقة لعناقيد الرطب ومتى كان فى حبة واحدة سمع سنابل فى كل سنبلة مائة ثم انظر إلى أرض البوادي وفتش ظاهرها وباطنها فتراها ترابا متشابها فإذا أنزل عليها الماء اهتزت وربت وأنبتت من كل زوج بهيج ألوانا مختلفة ونباتا متشابها وغير متشابه لكل واحد طعم وريح ولون وشكل يخالف الآخر فانظر إلى كثرتها واختلاف أصنافها وكثرة أشكالها ثم اختلاف طبائع النبات وكثرة م", "english": "For the beast has no power over that; but as for man, God created for him the power, then he disabled it and disbelieved in God's grace therein. So those are like cattle — nay, they are even further astray from the way {فَأُولَٰئِكَ كَالْأَنْعَامِ بَلْ هُمْ أَضَلُّ سَبِيلًا}.", "book_title": "Contemplation", "section_title_ar": "Section 1273", "section_title_en": "On Contemplating the Earth, Its Rivers, Mountains, and Minerals — Section 1273", "section_index": 1273, "anchors": "- Root: فكر (f-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: التفكر: تأمل القلب وتدبر الشيء، والفكر: إعمال النظر في الشيء\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الفكر: قوة العلم التي بها يدرك الإنسان، وقيل: هو ترتيب أمور معلومة للتأدي إلى مجهول\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): الفكر: تصرف القلب في النظر — literal: إعمال الروية؛ metaphorical: التفكر في ملكوت الله\n- Root: ارض (arḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الأرض: التي عليها الناس، وجمعها أرضون\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء، ويعبر بها عن أسفل الشيء\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): literal: الأرض المهاد والفراش؛ metaphorical: تذلل الأرض وسكونها\n- Root: دحا (d-ḥ-w) / مهد (m-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: مهد: وطأ المكان وهيأه، والمهاد: الفراش الموطأ\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): المهد: الموضع المهيأ للصبي، والمهاد: الأرض الموطأة\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): literal: فرش الأرض وتمهيدها؛ metaphorical: جعلها قارة ساكنة لا تميد\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — the interposed clause \"فانظر\" after \"إلى الأرض\" maintains grammatical continuity while allowing rhetorical pause.", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Contemplation'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "5": [{"arabic": "قوله أولئك أصحاب النار هم فيها خالدون وثلاث آيات من آخر سورة البقرة من قوله لله ما في السموات وما في الأرض إلى آخرها وقل هو الله أحد خمس عشرة مرة (3) وصف من ثوابه في الحديث ما يخرج عن الحصر وقال كرز بن وبرة وهو من الأبدال قلت للخضر عليه السلام علمني شيئا أعمله في كل ليلة فقال إذا صليت المغرب فقم إلى وقت صلاة العشاء مصليا من غير أن تكلم أحدا وأقبل على صلاتك التي أنت فيها وسلم من كل ركعتين واقرأ في كل ركعة فاتحة الكتاب مرة وقل هو الله أحد ثلاثا فإن فرغت من صلاتك انصرف إلى منزلك ولا ت", "english": "The Messenger of God (peace be upon him) said — according to what ʿĀ'ishah (may God be pleased with her) narrated: \"Verily, the most excellent of prayers in the sight of God is the Maghrib prayer. He has not exempted from it either the traveler or the resident. By it He opened the prayer of the night, and by it He sealed the prayer of the day. Whoever prays the Maghrib and prays after it two units (rakʿatayn), God will build for him a palace (qaṣr) in Paradise.\"", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 271", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 271: On the Virtue of the Maghrib Prayer and the Facilitating Causes for the Night Vigil (Qiyām al-Layl)", "section_index": 271, "anchors": "- Root: غرب (gh-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of disappearance, setting, and the west; al-ghurāb is the setting of the sun.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغرب: غيبوبة الشمس\" — the occultation of the sun; the Maghrib prayer is named for its time, marking the transition from the day's light into the night's dominion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the sun's setting; metaphorically (Majaz), it signifies a boundary or point of transition — here, the temporal boundary between the daytime prayers and the night vigil.\n- Root: رسل (r-s-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sending forth with gentleness and ease; a messenger (rasūl) is one dispatched.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الرسل: الانبعاث على التؤدة\" — the root denotes a measured, deliberate sending forth; from this derives the concept of the prophetic messenger.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أرسل الله في الأمم رسلا\" (God sent messengers to the nations). Metaphorical: \"أرسل الله عليهم العذاب\" (God loosed chastisement upon them) — here, the ḥadīth's \"أرسل\" (in the chain) is literal transmission.\n- Root: ركع (r-k-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Bowing; the physical posture of inclination in prayer.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الركوع: الانحناء\" — used literally for the prayer posture and metaphorically for humility and submission before God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيخ راكع: منحن من الكبر\" (an old man bowed with age). Metaphorical: \"لغبت الإبل حتى ركعت\" (camels fatigued until they knelt) — in the ḥadīth, \"ركعات\" (units of prayer) are named for the bowing that defines them.\n- Root: صلا (ṣ-l-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root of prayer (ṣalāh); also linked to the fire of kindling (taṣliyah).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الصلي الإيقاد بالنار\" — the root denotes kindling fire; the ṣalāh is so named for the \"burning\" of devotion or for the supplication that \"kindles\" the heart toward God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The term ṣalāh in the Sacred Law is a transferred (naql) technical designation for the specific acts of bowing, prostration, and recitation — distinct from its lexical sense of supplication (duʿā').\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object, as in \"لم يحطها عن مسافر ولا عن مقيم\" where the pronominal object (ها) is separated from the second prepositional phrase (عن مقيم) by the conjunction, maintaining grammatical cohesion through parallel structure.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "6": [{"arabic": "وتولى وقال عز وجل وإن ربك لذوا مغفرة للناس على ظلمهم ويقال أن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم لم يزل يسأل في أمته حتى قيل له أما ترضى وقد أنزلت عليك هذه الآية وإن ربك لذو مغفرة للناس ظلمهم (2) وفي تفسير قوله تعالى {ولسوف يعطيك ربك فترضى} قال لا يرضى محمد وواحد من أمته في النار وكان أبو جعفر محمد بن علي يقول أنتم أهل العراق تقولون أرجى آية في كتاب الله عز وجل قوله {قل يا عبادي الذين أسرفوا على أنفسهم لا تقنطوا من رحمة الله} الآية ونحن أهل البيت نقول أرجى آية في كتاب الله تعالى قوله تعالى {ولسوف يعطيك ربك فترضى} وأما الأخ", "english": "So it was said to him: \"And what is in it of hope?\" He said: \"This world, all of it, is little, and a man's provision from it is little, and his religion is little in comparison to his provision. So look at how God Most High sent down in it the longest verse, to guide His servant to the path of caution in preserving his wealth — so how could He not preserve his religion, which has no substitute for him?\"", "book_title": "Fear and Hope", "section_title_ar": "Section 1033", "section_title_en": "The Book of Hope (Kitāb al-Rajā') — Section 1033: The Second Art — Surveying the Verses and Reports Concerning Hope", "section_index": 1033, "anchors": "- Root: وجود (w-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"finding\" and \"existence\"; here in the sense of what is present and available.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Existence as that which is found and realized; in theological context, the given order of creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) sense of \"being found\"; metaphorical (majāz) extension to the entirety of created things.\n- Root: رجا (r-j-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To hope, to expect with desire; the opposite of despair (ya's).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hope as a theological virtue — the heart's expectation of divine mercy, balanced against fear (khawf).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: expectation of a desired outcome; metaphorical: the soul's reliance on divine generosity.\n- Root: غفر (gh-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To cover, to shield, to pardon; the root implies veiling the fault.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine forgiveness as the covering of sins and the effacing of their consequence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: covering; metaphorical: God's remission of punishment.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — used to emphasize exclusive attributes of divine mercy and forgiveness.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Fear and Hope'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "هذه الآية وإن ربك لذو مغفرة للناس ظلمهم (2) وفي تفسير قوله تعالى {ولسوف يعطيك ربك فترضى} قال لا يرضى محمد وواحد من أمته في النار وكان أبو جعفر محمد بن علي يقول أنتم أهل العراق تقولون أرجى آية في كتاب الله عز وجل قوله {قل يا عبادي الذين أسرفوا على أنفسهم لا تقنطوا من رحمة الله} الآية ونحن أهل البيت نقول أرجى آية في كتاب الله تعالى قوله تعالى {ولسوف يعطيك ربك فترضى} وأما الأخبار فقد روى أبو موسى عنه صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال أمتي أمة مرحومة لا عذاب عليها في الآخرة عجل الله عقابها في الدنيا الزلازل", "english": "So it was said to him: \"And what is in it of hope?\" He said: \"This world, all of it, is little, and a man's provision from it is little, and his religion is little in comparison to his provision. So look at how God Most High sent down in it the longest verse, to guide His servant to the path of caution in preserving his wealth — so how could He not preserve his religion, which has no substitute for him?\"", "book_title": "Fear and Hope", "section_title_ar": "Section 1033", "section_title_en": "The Book of Hope (Kitāb al-Rajā') — Section 1033: The Second Art — Surveying the Verses and Reports Concerning Hope", "section_index": 1033, "anchors": "- Root: وجود (w-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"finding\" and \"existence\"; here in the sense of what is present and available.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Existence as that which is found and realized; in theological context, the given order of creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) sense of \"being found\"; metaphorical (majāz) extension to the entirety of created things.\n- Root: رجا (r-j-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To hope, to expect with desire; the opposite of despair (ya's).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hope as a theological virtue — the heart's expectation of divine mercy, balanced against fear (khawf).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: expectation of a desired outcome; metaphorical: the soul's reliance on divine generosity.\n- Root: غفر (gh-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To cover, to shield, to pardon; the root implies veiling the fault.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine forgiveness as the covering of sins and the effacing of their consequence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: covering; metaphorical: God's remission of punishment.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — used to emphasize exclusive attributes of divine mercy and forgiveness.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Fear and Hope'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "8": [{"arabic": "غير معلوم قدره الله سبحانه فلا حيلة في دفعه بل كل شيء عنده بمقدار ولكل أجل كتاب ولذلك شكا نبي من الأنبياء من امرأة ظالمة مستولية على الخلق فأوحى الله إليه فر من قدامها حتى تنقضي أيامها أي ما قدرناه في الأزل لا سبيل إلى تغييره فاصبر حتى تنقضي المدة التي سبق القضاء بدوام إقبالها فيها ومهما", "english": "As for its being a harm upon the envied one in his religion and his worldly life — it is clear that the blessing does not depart from him through your envy. Rather, whatever Allah — exalted be He — has decreed of prosperity and blessing, it must endure until an appointed term unknown to us, which Allah — glorified be He — has measured. There is no stratagem to repel it, for everything with Him is by measure (miqdār), and for every term there is a book (kitāb). {«لكل أجل كتاب»} \"For every term there is a decree.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 725", "section_title_en": "The Remedy That Expels the Disease of Envy from the Heart — Section 725", "section_index": 725, "anchors": "- Root: حسد (ḥ-s-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning — wishing for removal of a blessing from its possessor; the root also carries the sense of \"grudging malice\" that consumes the envier himself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسد: تمني زوال نعمة من مستحق لها\" — Envy is the wish for the removal of a blessing from one who deserves it, sometimes accompanied by striving to effect its removal. The believer envies not but rather admires (ghibṭah); the hypocrite envies.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) — envy is directed at another's blessing; metaphorically (majāz), envy \"eats the body\" as fire eats wood, indicating its self-destructive spiritual physiology.\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart as that which is ever-turning, overturning, and fluctuating between states.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — To turn a thing from one face to another; the heart's diseases are its deviations from its original sound disposition (fiṭrah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — turning a thing over; metaphorical — \"رجل قلب\" (a man who turns matters over) i.e., one who examines affairs with penetrating insight, as the physician of hearts must do.\n- Root: نعم (n-ʿ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Blessing, well-being, graciousness; the state of being in a condition of good.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النعمة: الحالة الحسنة\" — Blessing is the good state; it is a genus term applying to both little and much. Divine blessings are distributed by divine decree (qadar), and envy is resentment against that distribution.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — the act of bestowing; metaphorical — \"خفت نعامتهم\" (their ostriches departed) i.e., their blessings vanished, illustrating that blessings flee from the envier while remaining with the envied.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic sentence structure here employs extended conditional and causal chains (law-lā, fa-in, fa-innaka) where the apodosis is delayed by embedded justificatory clauses, requiring the translator to preserve the logical subordination without collapsing the rhetorical tension.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "11": [{"arabic": "تعالى {إن الله لا يغير ما بقوم حتى يغيروا ما بأنفسهم} وإنما التغير تكثير الشهوات فهي مرتع الشياطين ومرعاهم فما دامت مخصبة لم ينقطع ترددهم وما داموا يترددون لم ينكشف للعبد جلال الله سبحانه وكان محجوبا عن لقائه وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم لولا أن الشياطين يحومون على قلوب بني آدم لنظروا إلى ملكوت السموات (3) فمن هذا الوجه صار الصوم باب العبادة وصار جنة وإذا عظمت فضيلته إلى هذا الحد فلا بد من بيان شروطه الظاهرة والباطنة بذكر أركانه وسننه وشروطه الباطنة ونبين ذلك بثلاثة فصول", "english": "To proceed: Fasting is one quarter of faith, by virtue of his saying, peace and blessings be upon him: \"Fasting is half of patience\" — and by virtue of his saying, peace and blessings be upon him: \"Patience is half of faith\" {«الإيمان»}. Then it is distinguished by a particular characteristic of its attribution to Allah the Exalted, above all the other pillars — for Allah the Exalted said, in what His Prophet narrated from Him, peace and blessings be upon him: \"Every good deed is multiplied tenfold up to seven hundred times, except fasting, for it is Mine, and I shall reward it.\" And Allah the Exalted has said: {«Only the patient shall be paid their reward in full without reckoning»} (Q 39:10). And fasting is half of patience — so its reward has surpassed the law of measure and reckoning. And sufficient for you in knowing its excellence is his saying, peace and blessings be upon him: \"By Him in whose hand is my soul, the change in the breath of the mouth of the fasting person is more fragrant to Allah than the scent of musk. Allah the Exalted says: 'He only abandons his appetite, his food, and his drink for My sake — so fasting is for Me, and I shall reward it.'\" And he said, peace and blessings be upon him: \"Paradise has a gate called al-Rayyān, through which none shall enter except the fasting ones.\" And the fasting person is promised the meeting with Allah the Exalted as a portion of his fasting. And he said, peace and blessings be upon him: \"For the fasting person there are two joys: a joy when he breaks his fast, and a joy when he meets his Lord.\" And he said, peace and blessings be upon him: \"Everything has a gate, and the gate of worship is fasting.\" And he said, peace and blessings be upon him: \"The sleep of the fasting person is worship.\" And Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated that he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"When the month of Ramaḍān enters, the gates of Paradise are opened, the gates of the Fire are closed, the devils are chained, and a caller calls out: 'O seeker of good, come forth! O seeker of evil, desist!'\" And Wakīʿ said concerning His saying, the Exalted: {«Eat and drink in satisfaction for what you sent forth in the days past»} (Q 69:24) — these are the days of fasting, for in them they abandoned eating and drinking. And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, gathered in the rank of divine boasting (mubāhāh) between renunciation of the world and fasting, saying: \"Allah the Exalted boasts to His angels of the young worshiper, saying: 'O young man who abandons his appetite for My sake, who devotes his youth to Me — you are to Me as some of My angels.'\" And he said, peace and blessings be upon him, concerning the fasting person: \"Allah the Exalted says: 'Look, O My angels, at My servant — he has abandoned his appetite, his pleasure, his food, and his drink for My sake.'\" And it was said concerning His saying, the Exalted: {«No soul knows what comfort has been concealed for them, as a reward for what they used to do»} (Q 32:17) — it was said: their deed was fasting, because He said: {«Only the patient shall be paid their reward in full without reckoning»} — so the reward of the fasting person is poured out upon him without measure, and is dealt out to him in bulk, not falling under any imagination or estimation.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 185", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Secrets of Fasting — Section 185: On the Excellence of Fasting and Its Distinction Among the Acts of Worship", "section_index": 185, "anchors": "- Root: صوم (ṣ-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — abstention from any act, whether eating, speaking, or walking; a horse that refrains from movement or fodder is called ṣā'im.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological/Kalam semantic nuance — \"الصوم في الأصل: الإمساك عن الفعل\" — fasting is fundamentally the restraint of the self from action; it is an inward act of the heart, invisible to creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical distinction — Haqiqah: the month of fasting, \"فمن شهد منكم الشهر فليصمه\" (Q 2:185); Majaz: \"صام الفرس على آريه\" — a horse standing still at its tether, refusing fodder, metaphorically \"fasting.\"\n- Root: عله (ʿ-l-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — to feed or pasture; related to cultivation and nourishment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological/Kalam semantic nuance — not directly attested in Mufradat for this root; the root pertains to the means by which the body is sustained, which is precisely what the fasting person abstains from.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical distinction — literal feeding and satiation; the fasting person's abstention from these becomes the very means of spiritual nourishment.\n- Root: الي (a-l-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — to convey a message; the angel (malak) is so named because the message is \"chewed\" (yu'lak) in the mouth, as a horse chews its bit.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological/Kalam semantic nuance — \"المألكة: الرسالة\" — the angelic function is the conveyance of divine communication; the fasting person's hunger narrows the channels through which the devil (who runs like blood through the veins) reaches the heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical distinction — literal conveyance of a message versus the metaphorical \"message\" of the devil's insinuation (waswās) through the pathways of the body.\n- Root: صبر (ṣ-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — to restrain, to hold back in constriction; \"صبرت الدابة: حبستها بلا علف\" — to hold an animal back without fodder.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological/Kalam semantic nuance — \"الصبر: الإمساك في ضيق\" — patience is restraint within constriction; it is the holding of the soul upon what reason and law require, or from what they require it to abstain. Its names vary by context: in battle it is courage, in calamity it is patience proper.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical distinction — Haqiqah: \"صبرت على ما أكره وصبرت عما أحب\" — patience upon the disliked and patience from the beloved; Majaz: \"صبرت يمينه\" — to bind one's oath severely, and \"هو أصبر على الضرب من الأرض\" — more enduring of striking than the earth.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle innamā restricts and confines the predicate exclusively to its subject, as in the divine saying {«إنما يوفى الصابرون أجرهم بغير حساب»} and {«إنما يذر شهوته وطعامه وشرابه لأجلي»}, where the restriction elevates the restricted matter to a rank beyond all quantitative measure.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "حسن التوفيق والتأييد والتسديد وإن الله لا يغير ما بقوم حتى يغيروا ما بأنفسهم فمن العبد المجاهدة ومن الله الهداية ومن العبد قرع الباب ومن الله فتح الباب والله لا يضيع أجر المحسنين وإن تك حسنة يضاعفها ويؤت من لدنه أجرا عظيما المقام الثاني في دفع العارض منه في أثناء العبادة وذلك لا بد من تعلمه أيضا فإن من جاهد نفسه وقلع مغارس الرياء من قلبه بالقناعة وقطع الطمع وإسقاط نفسه من أعين المخلوقين واستحقار مدح المخلوقين وذمهم فالشيطان لا يتركه في أثناء العبادات بل يعارضه بخطرات الرياء ولا تنقطع عنه نزعاته وهوى النفس وميلها لا ينمحي بالكلية", "english": "So when the harm and injury of these causes is firmly settled in his heart, his desire [for them] slackens, and his heart turns toward God. For the intelligent person does not covet that whose harm is abundant and whose benefit is scant. It suffices him that if people knew what is in his inner self—the intent of ostentation (riyā') and the display of sincerity—they would abhor him. And God will surely expose his secret until He makes him odious to the people and makes them know that he is a show-off, abhorrent to God. Had he been sincere to God, God would have unveiled his sincerity to them, made him beloved to them, subjected them to him, and loosened their tongues in praise and commendation of him—even though there is no perfection in their praise, nor deficiency in their blame. As the poet of Banī Tamīm said: \"My praise is an adornment, and my blame is a disgrace.\" The Messenger of God (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him: \"You have lied—that belongs to God, there is no deity but Him.\" // [Hadith: A poet of Banī Tamīm said, \"My praise is an adornment, and my blame is a disgrace.\" He (the Prophet) said: \"You have lied—that belongs to God.\" Ahmad narrated it from the hadith of al-Aqra' ibn Ḥābis—who was the speaker of that verse, excluding the phrase \"You have lied\"—and its narrators are trustworthy, except that I do not know of Abū Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān having heard [directly] from al-Aqra'. Al-Tirmidhī narrated it from the hadith of al-Barā' and deemed it sound (ḥasan), with the wording: \"A man said, 'Indeed, my praise...'\"]", "book_title": "Condemnation of Status and Ostentation", "section_title_ar": "Section 827", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 827: The Second Station: Repelling the Accidental Promptings of Ostentation During Worship", "section_index": 827, "anchors": "- Root: كذب (kadhaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: falsehood, lying, saying what is contrary to reality; also used for \"to declare someone a liar.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, kadhb is the antithesis of sidq (truthfulness); it is a moral and epistemic vice, often linked to hypocrisy (nifāq) and false claims.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): to report what did not occur. Metaphorical (majāz): attributing to God what is unworthy of Him, as in the Prophet's rebuke of the poet's self-praise.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Predicate Emphasis (Bāb al-Tawkīd) — The Prophet's reply \"كَذَبْتَ\" (You have lied) functions as an emphatic categorical refutation, structured as a nominal sentence with implied subject, establishing a permanent verdict rather than a transient rebuke.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Status and Ostentation'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "سفره ووقفة في حركته فإن الله لا يغير ما بقوم حتى يغيروا ما بأنفسهم وإذا زاغوا أزاغ الله قلوبهم وما الله بظلام للعبيد ولكنهم يظلمون أنفسهم ومن لم يؤهل للجولان في هذا الميدان والتطواف في متنزهات هذا البستان ربما سافر بظاهر بدنه في مدة مديدة فراسخ معدودة مغتنما بها تجارة للدنيا أو ذخيرة للآخرة فإن كان مطلبه العلم والدين أو الكفاية للاستعانة على الدين كان من سالكي سبيل الآخرة وكان له في سفره شروط وآداب إن أهملها كان من عمال الدنيا وأتباع الشيطان وإن واظب عليها لم يخل سفره عن فوائد تلحقه بعمال الآخرة ونحن نذكر آدابه وشروطه في بابين", "english": "And to this journey God — exalted be He — has summoned [us] by His saying: {«سَنُرِيهِمْ آيَاتِنَا فِي الْآفَاقِ وَفِي أَنْفُسِهِمْ»} [We shall show them Our signs in the horizons and in their own selves — Fussilat 41:53], and by His saying — exalted be He: {«وَفِي الْأَرْضِ آيَاتٌ لِلْمُوقِنِينَ * وَفِي أَنْفُسِكُمْ أَفَلَا تُبْصِرُونَ»} [And in the earth are signs for those who are certain; and in your own selves — do you not then see? — Dhāriyāt 51:20-21]. And upon sitting back from this journey, censure fell by His saying — exalted be He: {«وَإِنَّكُمْ لَتَمُرُّونَ عَلَيْهِمْ مُصْبِحِينَ * وَبِاللَّيْلِ أَفَلَا تَعْقِلُونَ»} [And indeed you pass by them in the morning, and by night — do you not then comprehend? — Ṣāffāt 37:137-138], and by His saying — exalted be He: {«وَكَأَيِّنْ مِنْ آيَةٍ فِي السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضِ يَمُرُّونَ عَلَيْهَا وَهُمْ عَنْهَا مُعْرِضُونَ»} [And how many a sign in the heavens and the earth do they pass by, while they are from it turning away — Yūsuf 12:105]. So whoever is facilitated this journey, he never ceases in his traveling to take pleasure in a Garden whose breadth is the heavens and the earth — while he is still with his body at rest, settled in his homeland.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Travel", "section_title_ar": "Section 464", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquettes of Travel — Being the Seventh Book of the Quarter of Customs from The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 464, "anchors": "- Root: سفر (s-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"unveiling/disclosure\" (kashf al-ghitā'); also denotes journeying across land, and a group of travelers (safar/qawm musāfirūn).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-safar: \"the removal of a covering,\" specialized to physical unveiling (e.g., removing a turban from the head). Its theological extension: the journey as a means of unveiling hidden realities — both outward horizons (āfāq) and inward selves (anfus).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): physical travel with the body, provisions for a journey (sufrah). Metaphorical (majāz): \"a face radiant with joy\" (wajh musfir); \"the morning brightening\" (asfara al-ṣubḥ) — the inner journey as illumination of the heart's countenance.\n- Root: وفي (w-f-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Fullness, completion, fulfillment of a measure or covenant.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-wāfī: \"that which has reached completion/perfection.\" Awfā bi-ʿahdihi: to fulfill one's covenant completely without breach. Theologically: the complete fulfillment of the servant's journey — arriving at the full measure of human perfection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a full-weight dirham, complete measure. Metaphorical: \"to reach completion of years\" (māta wa-ant bi-wafā'); \"to come upon/arrive at\" (awfā ʿalā) — the journey's end as arrival at full term.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, spirit; that which is precious and valued.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-nafs: the spirit (rūḥ); in God's speech \"He knows what is in your selves\" — the inner dimension of the human being, the seat of the divine secret.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a precious thing (shay' nafīs). Metaphorical: \"the soul's blood\" (nafsuhu = damuhu); the inner landscape (āfāq al-anfus) as the terrain of the inward journey.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Prepositional Interposition (al-faṣl bayna al-jār wa-al-majrūr) — governing the periodic suspension of meaning across clauses, as in the verse {سَنُرِيهِمْ آيَاتِنَا فِي الْآفَاقِ وَفِي أَنْفُسِهِمْ}, where the dual prepositional phrases (fī al-āfāq / fī anfusihim) are syntactically parallel yet semantically interposed, requiring the translator to preserve the balanced suspension of the two terrains of divine signs.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Travel'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وإلى حالك ويغبطونك فى حسنك وجمالك والملائكة يمشون بين يديك ومن خلفك وينادون على رءوس الأشهاد هذا فلان بن فلان رضى الله عنه وأرضاه وقد سعد سعادة لا يشقى بعدها أبدا أفترى أن هذا المنصب ليس بأعظم من المكانة التى تنالها فى قلوب الخلق فى الدنيا بريائك ومداهنتك وتصنعك وتزينك فإن كنت تعلم أنه خير منه بل لانسبة له إليه فتوسل إلى إدراك هذه الرتبة بالإخلاص الصافى والنية الصادقة فى معاملتك مع الله فلن تدرك ذلك إلا به وإن تكن الأخرى والعياذ بالله بأن خرج من صحيفتك جريمة كنت تحسبها هينة وهى عند الله عظيمة فمقتك لأجلها فقا", "english": "Know that none is saved from the peril of the Scale except him who, in this world, called his own soul to account, and weighed therein — by the scale of the Sacred Law — his deeds, his words, his passing thoughts, and his glances, just as ʿUmar — may God be well pleased with him — said: \"Call yourselves to account before you are called to account, and weigh yourselves before you are weighed.\" Indeed, his calling himself to account consists in his repenting, before death, from every act of disobedience with a sincere repentance (tawbah naṣūḥ), and in his making up for what was neglected of his shortcomings regarding the obligatory commands of God — may He be exalted — and in his restoring rights to those he wronged, grain by grain, and in his seeking absolution from everyone whom he harmed by his tongue, his hand, or his evil suspicion harbored in his heart — and this by winning their hearts' contentment — until he dies with no outstanding wrong against him, nor any neglected obligation.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1343", "section_title_en": "The Perils of the Scale and the Reckoning of Wrongs Against Others", "section_index": 1343, "anchors": "- Root: وزن (w-z-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is weighing, measuring, and balancing; al-mīzān (the Scale) is the instrument of equitable judgment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, al-mīzān is the divine instrument of justice on the Day of Resurrection, weighing deeds to manifest equitable recompense, not a corporeal balance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes physical weighing; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to moral and spiritual accounting, as in \"weighing one's deeds by the scale of the Sacred Law.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Conditional and Exceptive Constructions (الشّرط والاستثناء) — governing the protasis-apodosis structure in \"whoever's scales are heavy... whoever's scales are light,\" and the exceptive force in \"none is saved... except he who reckons.\"", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "الكعبة فقلت ومن هم قال الأكثرون أموالا إلا من قال هكذا وهكذا من بين يديه ومن خلفه وعن يمينه وعن شماله وقليل ما هم ما من صاحب إبل ولا بقر ولا غنم لا يؤدي زكاتها إلا جاءت يوم القيامة أعظم ما كانت وأسمنه تنطحه بقرونها وتطؤه بأظلافها كلما نفدت أخراها عادت عليه أولاها حتى يقضى بين الناس (1) وإذا كان هذا التشديد مخرجا في الصحيحين فقد صار من مهمات الدين الكشف عن أسرار الزكاة وشروطها الجلية والخفية ومعانيها الظاهرة والباطنة مع الاقتصار على ما لا يستغنى عن معرفته مؤدي الزكاة وقابضها وينكشف ذلك في أربعة فصول الفصل الأول في أنواع الزكاة وأسباب", "english": "To proceed: God Most High made alms (al-zakāh) one of the pillars of Islam, and joined its mention with prayer (al-ṣalāh), which is the loftiest of symbols. He said Most High: {«وَأَقِيمُوا الصَّلَاةَ وَآتُوا الزَّكَاةَ»} — \"And establish prayer and give alms.\" And he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Islam is built upon five: the testimony that there is no god but God and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger, the establishment of prayer, and the giving of alms.\" And He intensified the threat against those who fall short in it, saying: {«وَالَّذِينَ يَكْنِزُونَ الذَّهَبَ وَالْفِضَّةَ وَلَا يُنفِقُونَهَا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ فَبَشِّرْهُم بِعَذَابٍ أَلِيمٍ»} — \"And those who hoard gold and silver and do not spend them in the way of God — give them tidings of a painful chastisement.\" And the meaning of \"spending in the way of God\" is the payment of the due right of alms (zakāh).", "book_title": "Mysteries of Zakat", "section_title_ar": "Section 167", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Secrets of Alms (Zakat) — Section 167", "section_index": 167, "anchors": "- Root: زكا (Z-K-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Zakā al-zar'u yazkū zakā'an: it increased and grew.\" Core meaning: growth, increase, purification.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"The origin of zakāh is the growth that occurs through the blessing of God Most High... and from it is zakāh: what a person pays of God's right to the poor, so named for the hope of blessing in it, or for the purification (tazkiyah) of the soul, i.e., its growth.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): growth and increase. Metaphorical (majāz): the purification of wealth through payment of the obligatory due.\n- Root: نعم (N-'-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Na'ima 'ayshuhu: his life was pleasant.\" Core meaning: bounty, well-being, grace.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-Ni'mah: the good state... and al-Ni'mah (with kasrah) is used for the genus, said of both little and much.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"Jallat ni'mat Allāh wa na'mā'uh\" — God's grace and bounty are immense. Metaphorical: \"Khāfat na'āmatuhum\" — their ostriches departed (i.e., they fled), showing the root's extended usage.\n- Root: ظهر (Z-H-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Zahara: to become apparent, to be victorious.\" Core meaning: back, outward appearance, manifestation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-Zahr: the back (body part)... and it is used metaphorically for the surface of the earth.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"Rajul muzhir: a man strong of back.\" Metaphorical: \"Qalabtu al-amra zahrān li-baṭn\" — I turned the matter over back and belly (examined it thoroughly).\n- Root: سرر (S-R-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Sarra: to conceal, to keep secret.\" Core meaning: secret, inner self, joy.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-Isrār: the opposite of al-i'lān (declaring)... and al-sirr is the speech concealed in the soul.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"Asarra al-ḥadīth: he concealed the speech.\" Metaphorical: \"Aṭaytuka sirrah: I gave you its choicest part (the secret/core of something).\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-faṣl bayna al-jār wa al-majrūr (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object when the intervening clause is complete in itself, as in \"fa'āfa 'alayhim min ni'matihi mā...\" where the prepositional phrase is completed after an intervening relative clause.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Mysteries of Zakat'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "13": [{"arabic": "وإذا سمعت صوت الرعد فقل سبحان من يسبح الرعد بحمده والملائكة من خيفته (7) فإن رأيت الصواعق فقل اللهم لا تقتلنا بغضبك ولا تهلكنا بعذابك وعافنا قبل ذلك (8) قاله كعب فإذا أمطرت السماء فقل اللهم سقيا هنيئا وصيبا نافعا (9) اللهم اجعله صيب رحمة ولا تجعله صيب عذاب (10) فإذا غضبت فقل اللهم اغفر لي ذنبي وأذهب غيظ قلبي وأجرني من الشيطان الرجيم (1) فإذا خفت قوما فقل اللهم إنا نجعلك في نحورهم ونعوذ بك من شرورهم (2) فإذا غزوت فقل اللهم أنت عضدي ونصيري وبك أقاتل (3) وإذا طنت أذنك فصل على محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم", "english": "When you rise in the morning and hear the call to prayer (adhān), it is recommended for you to respond to the caller of prayer (mu'adhdhin) — and we have already mentioned this, along with the supplications for entering and leaving the privy, and the supplications of ablution (wuḍū'), in the Book of Purification. When you set out toward the mosque, say: \"O Allah, place light (nūr) in my heart, and light in my tongue, and place light in my hearing, and place light in my sight, and place light behind me, and light before me, and place light above me. O Allah, grant me light.\" And also say: \"O Allah, I ask You by the right of those who ask of You, and by the right of this my walking unto You — for I have not gone out in insolence, nor in vanity, nor for show, nor for reputation. I have gone out seeking protection from Your wrath and seeking Your good pleasure. So I ask You to deliver me from the Fire and to forgive me my sins — verily, none forgives sins but You.\"", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 254", "section_title_en": "Section 254 — Supplications for the Hours of Day and Night: From Dawn to Sleep", "section_index": 254, "anchors": "- Root: ن و ر (n-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النور: الضوء المنتشر الذي يُبصر به، وهو ضد الظلمة\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النور في القرآن يقع على ضروب: منها المعقول كالإيمان والحكمة، قال تعالى: {قد جاءكم من الله نور وكتاب مبين}، ومنها المحسوس كضوء الشمس والقمر\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): النور حقيقة في الضوء المحسوس، ومجاز في الهدى والإيمان والعلم — فدعاء النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم بطلب النور يشمل المعنيين جميعاً\n- Root: س ج د (s-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: السجود: وضع الجبهة على الأرض تعظيماً، وأصله التطامن والانخفاض\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): السجود ضربان: سجود عبادة وهو وضع الجبهة على الأرض تقرباً، وسجود طاعة وهو الانقياد والخضوع — قال تعالى: {ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعاً وكرهاً}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): سجد حقيقة في وضع الجبهة، ومجاز في الخضوع والانقياد — وقوله \"سجد لك سوادي وخيالي\" من المجاز المرسل\n- Root: ذ ك ر (dh-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الذكر: نقيض النسيان، والذكر: الصوت الذي يُذكر به الشيء، والذكر: الشرف والصيت\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الذكر ضربان: ذكر بالقلب وهو التفكر في عظمة الله، وذكر باللسان وهو التلفظ بأسمائه — وأعلى مراتبه ما اجتمع فيه القلب واللسان\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): الذكر حقيقة في استحضار الشيء بالقلب أو اللسان، ومجاز في الصلاة والدعاء والثناء\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — جواز تقديم المعمول وتأخيره مع بقاء العامل عاملاً في المعنى، كقوله \"لك الحمد\" حيث تقدم الجار والمجرور على المبتدأ للاختصاص والاهتمام", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "15": [{"arabic": "وهو أحد والآصال التي ذكرها الله تعالى إذ قال ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعا وكرها وظلالهم بالغدو والآصال وإذا سجد لله عز وجل الجمادات فكيف يجوز أن يغفل العبد العاقل عن أنواع العبادات الورد السادس إذا دخل وقت العصر دخل وقت الورد السادس وهو الذي أقسم الله تعالى به فقال تعالى {والعصر} هذا أحد معنيي الآية وهو المراد بالآصال في أحد التفسيرين وهو العشي المذكور في قوله {وعشيا} وفي قوله {بالعشي والإشراق} وليس في هذا الورد صلاة إلا أربع ركعات بين الأذان والإقامة كما سبق في الظهر ثم يصلي الفرض ويشتغل بالأقسام الأربعة المذكورة", "english": "I say: And just as knowledge and remembrance are the nourishment of the heart, which can never be cut off from it, so too the measure of moderation is this; and deficiency from it may lead to the disturbance of the body — except for one who accustoms himself to wakefulness gradually, for he may train himself upon it without disturbance. And this litany (wird) is among the longest of the litanies and the most delightful for the worshippers, and it is one of the \"evenings and mornings\" (al-āṣāl) which God Most High mentioned when He said: {«ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعا وكرها وظلالهم بالغدو والآصال»} \"And to God prostrates whoever is in the heavens and the earth, willingly or unwillingly, and their shadows, in the mornings and the evenings\" (Q. 13:15). And when inanimate objects prostrate to God, Mighty and Majestic, how then can the intelligent servant be heedless of the varieties of worship?", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 261", "section_title_en": "The Seventh Daily Litany (Al-Wird al-Sābiʿ): The Time of Sunset — The Completion of the Day's Litanies and Self-Accounting", "section_index": 261, "anchors": "- Root: ورد (warada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes arriving at water, seeking it, and drinking from it; also the portion or share of water allotted for drinking at a specific time.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الورود أصله: قصد الماء\" — the core meaning is the intentional approach to water; the litany (wird) is the appointed portion of devotional recitation, likened to the daily share of water that a person must take to sustain life.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the act of arriving at water. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ورد علي كتاب سرني مورده\" — a book arrived upon me whose arrival pleased me; likewise, the wird is the arrival of the appointed time for worship, and the soul's approach to the fountain of divine remembrance.\n- Root: عصر (ʿaṣr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes pressing, squeezing, and extracting; also the time of afternoon, so called because it is the pressing of the day's end.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The ʿaṣr is the latter part of the day; God swore by it in {«والعصر»} (Q. 103:1), indicating its great merit.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the afternoon time. Metaphorical: the pressing of one's life — as the day is pressed at its end, so the life of man is pressed at its term.\n- Root: غرب (gharaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes disappearance, absence, and setting; the sun's setting (ghurūb) is its concealment from the eye.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The setting of the sun marks the boundary between the day's works and the night's; it is the terminus of the visible world's diurnal cycle.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the sun's descent below the horizon. Metaphorical: the setting of the sun of life — the death of man, after which there is no rising.\n- Root: حاسب (ḥāsaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes reckoning, counting, and taking account; muḥāsabah is the mutual reckoning between two parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-accounting (muḥāsabat al-nafs) is the soul's reckoning with itself over its deeds before the Day of Reckoning, for \"one who reckons himself before he is reckoned\" is among the wise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the arithmetic counting of sums. Metaphorical: the soul's audit of its own works, weighing the day's deeds against the standard of the Sacred Law.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of the prepositional phrase from its governing verb, as in the construction \"فسبحان الله حين تمسون\" where the adverbial clause is interposed to emphasize the temporal specification of the act of glorification.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "16": [{"arabic": "ومتعلقة بقدرته تصديقا له في قوله تعالى الله خالق كل شيء وفي قوله تعالى والله خلقكم وما تعملون وفي قوله تعالى وأسروا قولكم أو اجهروا به إنه عليم بذات الصدور ألا يعلم من خلق وهو اللطيف الخبير أمر العباد بالتحرز في أقوالهم وأفعالهم وإسرارهم وإضمارهم لعلمه بموارد أفعالهم واستدل على العلم بالخلق وكيف لا يكون خالقا لفعل العبد وقدرته تامة لا قصور فيها وهي متعلقة بحركة أبدان العباد والحركات متماثلة وتعلق القدرة بها لذاتها فما الذي يقصر تعلقها عن بعض الحركات دون البعض مع تماثلها أو كيف يكون الحيوان مستبدا با", "english": "Rather, the attribute of origination (ḥudūth) is established for bodies only by virtue of their being subject to change and the fluctuation of states. So how could their Creator share with them in the acceptance of change? And upon this is built [the doctrine] that His Speech (kalām) is eternal, subsisting in His Essence—only the sounds indicating it are originated. Just as one rationally apprehends the subsistence of a request for learning and the father's will for [his] child in the father's essence before the child is created—such that when the child is created, attains reason, and God creates for him a knowledge pertaining to what is in his father's heart of the request, the child becomes commanded by that very request which subsisted in his father's essence and whose existence endured until the time of his child's awareness of it—so let one rationally apprehend the subsistence of the command indicated by His words, Mighty and Majestic: {«اخلع نعليك»} \"Take off your sandals\" [Q 20:12], in God's Essence, and Moses—peace be upon him—becoming addressed by it after his existence, when God created for him a knowledge of that command and hearing of that eternal Speech.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 92", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 92: On the Divine Attributes of Eternity, Knowledge, Will, and the Third Pillar Concerning God's Acts", "section_index": 92, "anchors": "- Root: قدم (q-d-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Primordial precedence; that which has no temporal beginning; eternal past (azal) and eternal future (abad).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Precedence either by time, by honor, or by ontological necessity—here, God's attributes are eternal in the sense that they are not subject to temporal origination.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"to precede.\" Metaphorical: that which cannot be conceived as non-existent posterior to its existence—applied to God's essence and attributes.\n- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Comprehension of a thing as it truly is; the indelible mark or trace by which one is guided.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its reality\"—twofold: apprehension of the essence, and judgment concerning existence or non-existence. God's knowledge is eternal, encompassing all created things without renewal.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: perception and awareness. Metaphorical: the \"signs\" (maʿālim) of God's power—His knowledge is identical with His creative act, for \"Does He who created not know?\" {«ألا يعلم من خلق»}.\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measuring, proportioning, then bringing into existence upon a determined measure.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Origination from nothing without prior model—\"He created the heavens and the earth\" {«خلق السماوات والأرض»}—and also bringing forth from something, as man from a drop. All acts of creatures are God's creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the tailor's measuring before cutting. Metaphorical: God's bringing forth existence upon a measure dictated by wisdom—He is the sole Creator (al-Khāliq) of all acts, including those of His servants.\n- Root: قتل (q-t-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Removal of the spirit from the body; also, intense subjugation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The root meaning is the separation of soul from body, whether by an agent (qatl) or by natural death (mawt). Used here analogically: as \"killer,\" \"killed,\" and \"killing\" are mutually entailing, so are \"knower,\" \"known,\" and \"knowledge.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: slaying. Metaphorical: mutual entailment—just as no killer exists without a killing and a killed one, so no knower exists without knowledge and a known object.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب التلازم والتضايف (Mutual Entailment and Correlativity) — Terms like ʿālim/ʿilm/maʿlūm are correlatives (mutalāzimāt) that cannot be conceptually separated; the predicate necessitates the co-existence of its correlates in the intellect.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "عاجزون عن أنفسهم لا يملكون لأنفسهم نفعا ولا ضرا فكيف يملكون لغيرهم هذا في الدنيا فكيف في يوم لا يجزي والد عن ولده ولا مولود هو جاز عن والده شيئا بل تقول الأنبياء فيه نفسي نفسي فكيف يستبدل الجاهل عن ثواب الآخرة ونيل القرب عند الله ما يرتقبه بطمعه الكاذب في الدنيا من الناس فلا ينبغي أن نشك في أن المرائي بطاعة الله في سخط الله من حيث النقل والقياس جميعا هذا إذا لم يقصد الأجر فأما إذا قصد الأجر والحمد جميعا في صدقته أو صلاته فهو الشرك الذي يناقض الإخلاص وقد ذكرنا حكمه في كتاب الإخلاص ويدل على ما نقل", "english": "The Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, named it the lesser shirk (al-shirk al-aṣghar). The hadith naming riya' as the lesser shirk was transmitted by Ahmad from the hadith of Mahmud ibn Labid, and it has already preceded. Al-Tabarani also narrated it from the transmission of Mahmud ibn Labid from Rafi' ibn Khadij, making it part of Rafi's musnad, and it has also preceded recently. Al-Hakim recorded it and authenticated its chain from the hadith of Shaddad ibn Aws: \"We used to count riya' on the day of the Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, as the lesser shirk.\"", "book_title": "Contemplation", "section_title_ar": "Section 817", "section_title_en": "The Degrees of Riya' (Show) — Section 817", "section_index": 817, "anchors": "- Root: راءى (rā'ā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of visual display, showing oneself; from الرؤية (seeing), implying making oneself seen.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Riya' is the seeking of station (manzilah) in hearts through acts of obedience — a subtle shirk because the actor's qasd (intention) is diverted from the Creator to creatures.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) = mutual seeing; metaphorical (majaz) = displaying deeds for the gaze of others, transferring the object of worship from God to His servants.\n- Root: قصد (qaṣada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To aim, intend, direct one's course toward a target; the straight path of intention.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qasd is the straightness of a road; iqtisad (moderation) is praiseworthy between two extremes — here, the qasd of the actor is the very substance of the act's moral weight.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal = aiming at a physical target; metaphorical = the soul's inclination toward a goal, whether divine reward or human praise.\n- Root: عظم (ʿaẓuma)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be great, to magnify; ʿazamah = grandeur, majesty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): ʿIẓam (magnification) is the heart's ascription of greatness to an object; when transferred from God to creatures, it constitutes the hidden kernel of shirk.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal = physical largeness; metaphorical = the heart's veneration (taʿẓīm) of a being, which is the true object of worship.\n- Root: درج (daraja)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To ascend step by step; degrees of a ladder.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Darajah is a station considered by ascent; used for ranks of honor or degradation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal = physical steps; metaphorical = graded levels of moral severity, as here: \"degrees of riya'.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين المبتدأ والخبر (Interposition between subject and predicate) — The periodic structure \"اعلم أن بعض أبواب الرياء أشد وأغلظ من بعض\" employs an embedded clause (أنّ + nominal sentence) requiring careful tracking of the governed accusative and its predicate to preserve the logical hierarchy of the author's taxonomy.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Contemplation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "17": [{"arabic": "أنه أريد به غير الظاهر كما ورد في تفسير قوله تعالى أنزل من السماء ماء فسالت أودية بقدرها الآية وأن معنى الماء ههنا هو القرآن ومعنى الأودية هي القلوب وأن بعضها احتملت شيئا كثيرا وبعضها قليلا وبعضها لم يحتمل والزبد مثل الكفر والنفاق فإنه وإن ظهر وطفا على رأس الماء فإنه لا يثبت والهداية التي تنفع الناس تمكث وفي هذا القسم تعمق جماعة فأولوا ما ورد في الآخرة من الميزان والصراط وغيرهما وهو بدعة إذ لم ينقل ذلك بطريق الرواية وإجراؤه على الظاهر غير محال فيجب إجراؤه على الظاهر", "english": "The third category is when a thing is such that if it were mentioned explicitly it would be understood and there would be no harm in it, yet one alludes to it by way of metaphor (istiʿārah) and symbol (ramz), so that its impact upon the heart of the listener may be more powerful, and so that there may be a benefit in magnifying the matter's importance in his heart. For example, if someone said, \"I saw so-and-so hanging pearls upon the necks of swine,\" he would be alluding thereby to the dissemination of knowledge and the scattering of wisdom among those unworthy of it. Now, the listener may have his understanding rush to the literal sense of the words; but the verifier (muḥaqqiq), when he reflects and knows that that man had no pearls with him, nor was there any swine in that place, becomes alert to the apprehension of the secret and the inner meaning. Thus people differ in this matter. Of this kind is the poet's saying:", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 84", "section_title_en": "The Third Category of Allusion: Metaphorical Expression for Deeper Impact", "section_index": 84, "anchors": "- Root: كنو (K-N-W)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To use a metonymy or indirect expression; to refer to something by a name other than its direct designation\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The use of indirect expression to convey a meaning whose direct statement, while comprehensible, would lack the desired psychological impact\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes between literal expression (haqīqah) and metaphorical indirection (kināyah) — here the latter is employed for rhetorical amplification\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn over, to overturn, to transform from one state to another\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart as the seat of understanding and the organ of spiritual perception; the \"turning\" of meanings upon the heart\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to flip a thing over. Metaphorical: the heart's reception and internalization of meaning — \"وقع في قلبه\" (its impact upon his heart)\n- Root: مثل (M-TH-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Likeness, similitude, example, parable\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The expression of a meaning through an image that contains either the very essence of that meaning or its likeness\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: resemblance. Metaphorical: the parabolic mode where the image conveys the inner reality\n- Root: ظهر (Z-H-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The back; the outward, apparent aspect of a thing\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The apparent sense (zāhir) versus the inner meaning (bāṭin); the exoteric versus the esoteric\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the visible surface. Metaphorical: \"ظهرًا لبطن\" — turning a matter over, examining both its outward and inward aspects\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Specification) — The structure \"إنما\" in the Quranic citation restricts and focuses the predication, indicating that the creative command is exclusively and entirely Allah's, with no intermediary or temporal process.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "19": [{"arabic": "لكل عبد منيب} وقال عز وجل {وما يتذكر إلا من ينيب} وقال تعالى {إنما يتذكر أولوا الألباب} فالذي آثر غرور الدنيا على نعيم الآخرة فليس من ذوي الألباب ولذلك لا تنكشف له أسرار الكتاب رابعها أن يكون قد قرأ تفسيرا ظاهرا واعتقد أنه لا معنى لكلمات القرآن إلا ما تناوله النقل عن ابن عباس ومجاهد وغيرهما وأن ما وراء ذلك تفسير بالرأي وأن من فسر القرآن برأيه فقد تبوأ مقعده من النار فهذا أيضا من الحجب العظيمة وسنبين معنى التفسير بالرأي في الباب الرابع وأن ذلك لا يناقض قول علي رضي الله عنه إلا أن يؤتى الله عبدا فهما في القرآن وأنه لو كان ا", "english": "I say: These meanings are buried treasures that are not unveiled except to the divinely aided ones. To this 'Ali — may God be pleased with him — alluded by his saying: \"The Messenger of God — may God's peace and blessings be upon him — did not confide to me anything that he concealed from the people, except that God — mighty and majestic — grants to a servant understanding (fahm) of His Book. So let one be eager in seeking that understanding.\" (1) And Ibn Mas'ud — may God be pleased with him — said: \"Whoever desires the knowledge of the first and the last, let him stir up the Qur'an.\" And the greatest of the sciences of the Qur'an lie beneath the names of God — mighty and majestic — and His attributes, for most of creation have grasped from them only matters befitting their understandings and have not stumbled upon their depths.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 228", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 228: On the Inner Meanings of the Qur'an and the Removal of Obstacles to Understanding", "section_index": 228, "anchors": "- Root: فهم (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Comprehension, discernment, the faculty by which one grasps meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — a disposition by which one verifies the realities of what is perceived; distinct from mere hearing or transmission\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: swift apprehension; Metaphorical: the divine gift of insight into hidden meanings, as in the verse {ففهمناها سليمان}\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Turning, overturning, transformation from one state to another\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart as that which is perpetually turned and overturned between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical turning; Metaphorical: the heart as the seat of understanding that is veiled or illuminated, rusted or polished\n- Root: سمع (s-m-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hearing, the auditory faculty\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السمع: قوة في الأذن به يدرك الأصوات\" — extends metaphorically to comprehension and obedience; \"أو ألقى السمع وهو شهيد\" — one who casts his hearing while present\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: auditory perception; Metaphorical: attentive obedience and receptive understanding\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — governing the precise delimitation of predicates such as \"إنما يتذكر أولوا الألباب\" where restriction confines the action to its proper subject", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "21": [{"arabic": "فهو في النار وقاض قضى بغير ما أمر الله به فهو في النار (2) وقال كعب رحمه الله يكون في آخر الزمان علماء يزهدون الناس في الدنيا ولا يزهدون ويخوفون الناس ولا يخافون وينهون عن غشيان الولاة ويأتونهم ويؤثرون الدنيا على الآخرة يأكلون بألسنتهم يقربون الأغنياء دون الفقراء يتغايرون على العلم كما تتغاير النساء على الرجال يغضب أحدهم على جليسه إذا جالس غيره أولئك الجبارون أعداء الرحمن وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الشيطان ربما يسوفكم بالعلم فقيل يا رسول الله وكيف ذلك قال صلى الله عليه وسلم يقول اطلب الع", "english": "Know that the scholar who does not act upon his knowledge is condemned by the Book, the Sunnah, and the sayings of the sages. God, exalted be He, said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا لم تقولون ما لا تفعلون * كبر مقتا عند الله أن تقولوا ما لا تفعلون»} (\"O you who believe, why do you say what you do not do? Most hateful it is to God that you say what you do not do\"). And He said: {«أتأمرون الناس بالبر وتنسون أنفسكم»} (\"Do you command people to righteousness while you forget yourselves?\"). And He said: {«ومن الناس من يعجبك قوله في الحياة الدنيا ويشهد الله على ما في قلبه وهو ألد الخصام»} (\"And among people is he whose speech pleases you in the worldly life, and he calls God to witness what is in his heart, yet he is the fiercest of opponents\").", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 54", "section_title_en": "Section 54", "section_index": 54, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "22": [{"arabic": "قال الله تعالى وأنفقوا مما رزقناهم سرا وعلانية ندب إلى العلانية أيضا لما فيها من فائدة الترغيب فليكن العبد دقيق التأمل في وزن هذه الفائدة بالمحذور الذي فيه فإن ذلك يختلف بالأحوال والأشخاص فقد يكون الإعلان في بعض الأحوال لبعض الأشخاص أفضل ومن عرف الفوائد والغوائل ولم ينظر بعين الشهوة اتضح له الأولى والأليق بكل حال الوظيفة الخامسة أن لا يفسد صدقته بالمن والأذى قال الله تعالى لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى واختلفوا في حقيقة المن والأذى فقيل المن أن يذكرها والأذى أن يظهرها وقال سفيان من من فسدت", "english": "A group of the Followers (tābiʿūn) held that there are rights in wealth beyond the zakat — among them al-Nakhaʿī, al-Shaʿbī, ʿAṭāʾ, and Mujāhid. Al-Shaʿbī said, after being asked, \"Is there a right in wealth beyond the zakat?\": \"Yes. Have you not heard His saying — mighty and majestic is He: {«وَآتَى الْمَالَ عَلَىٰ حُبِّهِ ذَوِي الْقُرْبَىٰ»} 'And gives wealth, despite his love for it, to relatives' (2:177)?\" They also adduced His saying — mighty and majestic is He: {«وَمِمَّا رَزَقْنَاهُمْ يُنفِقُونَ»} 'And from what We have provided them, they spend' (2:3), and His saying — exalted is He: {«وَأَنفِقُوا مِن مَّا رَزَقْنَاكُم»} 'And spend from what We have provided you' (2:254). They claimed that this is not abrogated by the verse of zakat; rather, it falls within the right of a Muslim upon a Muslim. Its meaning is that it is incumbent upon the affluent one, whenever he finds a needy person, to remove his need — over and above the zakat wealth.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Zakat", "section_title_ar": "Section 173", "section_title_en": "The Second Division: Those Who Guard Their Wealth Awaiting Seasons of Need — On the Inner Meanings of Zakat (Section 173)", "section_index": 173, "anchors": "- Root: ف-ق-ر (f-q-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of neediness, poverty; the hollow of the spine (faqār) — that which is structurally weak and dependent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Poverty (faqr) has four senses: (1) essential neediness before Allah, universal to all creatures; (2) lack of possessions; (3) [the third and fourth senses as elaborated in the full lexicon entry].\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): one who lacks wealth. Metaphorical (majāz): \"the poverty of speech\" (fuqar al-kalām) — its subtle points, likened to ornaments shaped like vertebrae.\n- Root: ص-د-ق (ṣ-d-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Truthfulness in speech; giving (ṣadaqah) as the outward verification of inward faith.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ṣidq (truth) and kidhb (falsehood) are originally in speech — statement of what is, or what will be. By extension, ṣadaqah is that which verifies the truthfulness of one's claim to faith through expenditure.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: truthful speech. Metaphorical: \"a horse with true running\" (dhū miṣdaq fī al-jary) — that which verifies its excellence through action. Thus charity verifies the giver's sincerity.\n- Root: م-ن-ن (m-n-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The bestowal of a favor; reproaching another by reminding them of one's gift (al-mann).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [As elaborated in the full lexicon entry on the heart's self-perception of superiority over the recipient.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a heavy weight; a favor. Metaphorical: the act of counting one's favors over another — a corrosion of the gift's value.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — governing the precise scope of obligation (farḍ kifāyah) versus supererogatory merit (faḍl), and the restriction of \"rights in wealth\" (ḥuqūq fī al-māl) beyond the obligatory zakat.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Zakat'", "badge": "Insight"}], "25": [{"arabic": "فهو في النار وقاض قضى بغير ما أمر الله به فهو في النار (2) وقال كعب رحمه الله يكون في آخر الزمان علماء يزهدون الناس في الدنيا ولا يزهدون ويخوفون الناس ولا يخافون وينهون عن غشيان الولاة ويأتونهم ويؤثرون الدنيا على الآخرة يأكلون بألسنتهم يقربون الأغنياء دون الفقراء يتغايرون على العلم كما تتغاير النساء على الرجال يغضب أحدهم على جليسه إذا جالس غيره أولئك الجبارون أعداء الرحمن وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الشيطان ربما يسوفكم بالعلم فقيل يا رسول الله وكيف ذلك قال صلى الله عليه وسلم يقول اطلب الع", "english": "Know that the scholar who does not act upon his knowledge is condemned by the Book, the Sunnah, and the sayings of the sages. God, exalted be He, said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا لم تقولون ما لا تفعلون * كبر مقتا عند الله أن تقولوا ما لا تفعلون»} (\"O you who believe, why do you say what you do not do? Most hateful it is to God that you say what you do not do\"). And He said: {«أتأمرون الناس بالبر وتنسون أنفسكم»} (\"Do you command people to righteousness while you forget yourselves?\"). And He said: {«ومن الناس من يعجبك قوله في الحياة الدنيا ويشهد الله على ما في قلبه وهو ألد الخصام»} (\"And among people is he whose speech pleases you in the worldly life, and he calls God to witness what is in his heart, yet he is the fiercest of opponents\").", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 54", "section_title_en": "Section 54", "section_index": 54, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "يسعون في قطع ما أمر الله به أن يوصل ويفسدون في الأرض وقال تعالى {إنما السبيل على الذين يظلمون الناس ويبغون في الأرض بغير الحق} والنمام منهم وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن من شرار الناس", "english": "And whoever is the recipient of tale-bearing — it being said to him: \"So-and-so said such-and-such about you,\" or \"He did such-and-such against you,\" or \"He is scheming to corrupt your affair,\" or \"He is conspiring with your enemy,\" or \"He is disparaging your condition,\" or whatever runs in that course — then six matters are incumbent upon him:", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 692", "section_title_en": "Section 692 — On the Duties of the One to Whom Tale-Bearing (Namīmah) Is Conveyed", "section_index": 692, "anchors": "- Root: ن-م-م (N-M-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning — to stir up, incite, or carry tales; the one who transmits speech to cause discord.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — namīmah is a species of corrupt speech that severs what God has commanded to be joined; it is aligned with fāsiq (transgressor) status, rendering the bearer's testimony rejected.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — literally, the act of carrying and dropping seeds (naml) to cause sprouting; metaphorically, the dropping of speech into hearts to sow enmity.\n- Root: غ-ي-ب (Gh-Y-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning — that which is concealed from the senses.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — ghaybah (backbiting) is the mention of a person in his absence with what he dislikes; it is a violation of the unseen honor of the brother.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — literal absence from sight; metaphorical — the absence of the subject from the gathering while his reputation is being torn.\n- Root: ف-س-ق (F-S-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning — to depart, to deviate from the straight path.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — fisq is the breaking of the command of God; the fāsiq's report is not to be trusted without verification, per {إِن جَاءَكُمْ فَاسِقٌ بِنَبَإٍ فَتَبَيَّنُوا}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — literal: the date that peels from its husk; metaphorical: the soul that peels from obedience.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The verse {إِنَّمَا السَّبِيلُ عَلَى الَّذِينَ يَظْلِمُونَ النَّاسَ} restricts the path of blame exclusively to the oppressors, thereby excluding the tale-bearer's victim from any culpability and affirming the tale-bearer's full liability.", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "26": [{"arabic": "{ورضوا بالحياة الدنيا واطمأنوا بها} وقال تعالى {وما الحياة الدنيا في الآخرة إلا متاع} وقال تعالى {اعلموا أنما الحياة الدنيا لعب ولهو وزينة وتفاخر بينكم وتكاثر في الأموال والأولاد} الآية وكل ذلك ذم لها فنسأل الله السلامة فأولو الحزم من أرباب القلوب جربوا قلوبهم في حال الفرح بمؤاتاة الدنيا فوجدوها قاسية نفرة بعيدة التأثر عن ذكر الله واليوم الآخر وجربوها في حالة الحزن فوجدوها لينة رقيقة صافية قابلة لأثر الذكر فعلموا أن النجاة في الحزن الدائم والتباعد من أسباب الفرح والبطر ففطموها عن ملاذها وعودوها الصبر عن شهواتها حلالها وحرامها وعل", "english": "And beyond it lie afflictions greater than this—namely, that the soul rejoices in comfort within this world, leans toward it, and becomes tranquil in it with insolence (asharan) and pride (baṭaran), until it becomes intoxicated like a drunkard who does not awaken from his intoxication. And that joy in this world is a deadly poison that courses through the veins, expelling from the heart the fear (khawf) of God, sorrow (ḥuzn), the remembrance of death, and the terrors of the Day of Resurrection. This is the death of the heart. God, Exalted is He, said: {وَرَضُوا بِالْحَيَاةِ الدُّنْيَا وَاطْمَأَنُّوا بِهَا} (\"And they were pleased with the life of this world and felt secure in it\") (Q 10:7). And He, Exalted is He, said: {وَمَا الْحَيَاةُ الدُّنْيَا فِي الْآخِرَةِ إِلَّا مَتَاعٌ} (\"And the life of this world, compared to the Hereafter, is but a [passing] enjoyment\") (Q 13:26). And He, Exalted is He, said: {اعْلَمُوا أَنَّمَا الْحَيَاةُ الدُّنْيَا لَعِبٌ وَلَهْوٌ وَزِينَةٌ وَتَفَاخُرٌ بَيْنَكُمْ وَتَكَاثُرٌ فِي الْأَمْوَالِ وَالْأَوْلَادِ} (\"Know that the life of this world is but amusement and diversion and adornment and boasting among you and competition in increase of wealth and children\") (Q 57:20)—to the end of the verse. All of this is censure of it. So we ask God for safety (salāmah).", "book_title": "Breaking the Two Desires", "section_title_ar": "Section 629", "section_title_en": "On the Afflictions of Permissible Things and the Discipline of the Soul", "section_index": 629, "anchors": "- Root: س ك ت (s-k-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: silence, stillness, cessation of speech.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: silence (sukūt) as a positive act of worship when it restrains the tongue from falsehood and excess, aligning with the Quranic command to guard one's speech.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical: Literal silence (ḥaqīqah) is the absence of audible speech; metaphorical (majāz) silence is the restraint of the heart from vain desires and the tongue from all but the remembrance of God.\n- Root: ش ه و (sh-h-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: desire, longing, appetite; the inclination of the soul toward what pleases it.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: shahwah (appetite/desire) is a neutral faculty; its blameworthiness lies in its direction toward the forbidden (ḥarām) or in its excess beyond the measure of necessity (ḍarūrah), which enslaves the heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical: Literal desire is the physical craving for food, drink, or sensual pleasure; metaphorical desire is the soul's craving for status, comfort, and the fleeting adornments of this world (dunyā), which is the root of spiritual ruin.\n- Root: ف ط م (f-ṭ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: to wean, to cut off from suckling; to abstain from a habit.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: fiṭām (weaning) is used analogically for the discipline of the soul—cutting it off from worldly pleasures (shahawāt) to accustom it to the remembrance of God, just as a child is weaned from the breast.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical: Literal weaning is the physical act of separating an infant from milk; metaphorical weaning is the soul's training (riyāḍah) to abandon the \"milk\" of worldly comfort for the \"food\" of spiritual devotion.\n- Root: أ ن س ('-n-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: to be sociable, familiar, at ease; to find comfort in company.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: uns (intimacy/familiarity) with God is the highest station of the heart, achieved only after severing intimacy with the world; it is the soul's true home, contrasted with the alienation (waḥshah) of worldly attachment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical: Literal intimacy is human companionship; metaphorical intimacy is the heart's tranquil repose (ṭuma'nīnah) in the remembrance of God, as cited in the Quranic verse {أَلَا بِذِكْرِ اللَّهِ تَطْمَئِنُّ الْقُلُوبُ}.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of a preposition from its object for rhetorical emphasis, as in the periodic structure of the author's conditional clauses (e.g., \"فمن أراد... فحقه أن يلزمه\"), where the protasis is extended to clarify the subject's intent before the apodosis delivers the ruling.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Breaking the Two Desires'", "badge": "Insight"}], "28": [{"arabic": "ويدل على ذلك قوله تعالى {ألا بذكر الله تطمئن القلوب} وقوله تعالى {مثاني تقشعر منه جلود الذين يخشون ربهم ثم تلين جلودهم وقلوبهم إلى ذكر الله} وكل ما يوجد عقيب السماع في النفس فهو وجد فالطمأنينة والاقشعرار والخشية ولين القلب كل ذلك وجد وقد قال الله تعالى {إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم} وقال تعالى {لو أنزلنا هذا القرآن على جبل لرأيته خاشعا متصدعا من خشية الله} فالوجل والخشوع وجد من قبيل الأحوال وإن لم يكن من قبيل المكاشفات ولكن قد يصير سببا للمكاشفات والتنبيهات ولهذا قال صلى الله عليه وسلم زينوا القرآن بأ", "english": "**QUAD_ANCHORS:**\n- Root: وجد (Wajd)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root وجد encompasses multiple senses: finding/attaining (إدراك), intense love (الحب الشديد), grief (الحزن), and wealth/sufficiency (الغنى). Al-Khalil notes the range: \"الوجد: الحب. والوجد: الحزن. والوجد: الغنى. والوجد: إدراك الشيء\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجد: إدراك الشيء على خلاف ما يطلب\" — and in the technical terminology of the Sufis: \"ما يصادف القلب من واردات الأحوال\" (what the heart encounters of the influxes of spiritual states)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal sense is finding/attaining; metaphorical extension (majāz) in devotional context: \"وجد فلان في العبادة وجدا: إذا أتى بها على وجهها مع حضور القلب\"\n- Root: طبع (Tabʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الطبع: السجية التي جبل عليها الإنسان\" — the innate disposition upon which one is created; also \"الختم\" (sealing)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الطبع: السجية التي جبل عليها الإنسان\" — the original constitution\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes \"مطبوع\" (innate/natural) from \"متكلف\" (deliberately acquired) — \"فلان مطبوع على الكرم، ومتكلف له\"\n- Root: كلف (Kulfah/Takalluf)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"التكلف: تجشم ما لا يلزمك\" — undertaking what is not incumbent upon you with effort and hardship\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التكلف: تجشم ما ليس من شأنه\" — burdening oneself with what is not one's natural concern\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تكلفت الأمر: تجشمته على مشقة\" — deliberate self-imposition of effort\n- Root: سمع (Samāʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Auditory perception; also what is heard — recitation, song, remembrance\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السماع عند الصوفية: ما يهيج الوجد من الأصوات الموزونة\" — that which stirs ecstatic finding from measured sounds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"السماع عند القوم: حضور مجلس يهيج الأحوال الشريفة\" — attending an assembly that stirs noble spiritual states\n- Root: عشق (ʿIshq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"العشق: فرط الحب\" — excessive love; from \"العشقة\" — a plant that twines around another\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العشق: فرط الحب، وأصله من عشقة النبات وهي ما التف منه على غيره\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"العشق مجاز في حق الله تعالى\" — metaphorical when applied to God\n- Root: بكى (Bukāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Weeping, shedding tears\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البكاء: إجراء الدمع من عين لحزن أو غيره\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تباكى: تكلف البكاء\" — deliberate self-induced weeping\n- Root: صعق (Ṣaʿq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الصعق: الغشي. والصعق: الموت\" — swooning/loss of consciousness; death\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصعق: غشية تعرض من صوت شديد أو هول\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"صعق من هول الأمر: إذا غشي عليه من شدة الخوف\"\n- Root: خشع (Khushūʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الخشوع: الخضوع والتذلل\" — humility and submissiveness\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخشوع: التذلل والخضوع، وقيل: الخشوع في الصوت والبصر، والخضوع في البدن\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"خشعت الأصوات: سكنت. وخشع البصر: انكسر\"\n- Root: أنس (Uns)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الأنس: نقيض الوحشة\" — the opposite of loneliness/estrangement\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأنس: حضور القلب مع المحبوب\" — the heart's presence with the Beloved\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الأنس بالله: سكون القلب إليه\"\n- Root: وجل (Wajal)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الوجل: الخوف مع اضطراب القلب\" — fear accompanied by cardiac agitation\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: الخوف مع اضطراب القلب\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الوجل: أشد من الخوف، فإنه مع اضطراب\"\n- Root: طمأن (Ṭumaʾnīnah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الطمأنينة: السكون\" — tranquility\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الطمأنينة: سكون القلب بعد اضطراب\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"اطمأن القلب: سكن بعد اضطراب\"\n- Root: قشعر (Iqshirʿār)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"القشعريرة: تقبض الجلد من الخوف\" — contraction of the skin from fear\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الاقشعرار: انقباض الجلد وتقبضه من الخوف\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"اقشعر الجلد من الخوف: إذا تقبض\"\n- Root: لين (Līn)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"اللين: ضد الخشونة\" — softness, opposite of hardness\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اللين: نقيض الخشونة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"لان قلبه: إذا سكن وخشع\"\n- Root: غشي (Ghashy)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الغشي: الإغماء\" — loss of consciousness\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغشية: ما يغشى القلب من شدة الوجد\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"غشي عليه: إذا أغمي عليه من شدة وجد أو خوف\"\n- Root: زعق (Zaʿq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الزعق: الصياح الشديد\" — violent screaming\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الزعق: الصياح الشديد\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"زعق: صاح بأعلى صوته\"\n- Root: أزيز (Azīz)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الأزيز: صوت النار والمرجل\" — the sound of fire and boiling pot\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الأزيز: صوت النار والمرجل ونحوهما\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"أزيز الصدر: صوت يسمع من شدة البكاء والوجد\"\n- Root: شهق (Shahīq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الشهيق: رد النفس إلى الداخل بصوت\" — drawing breath inward with sound\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الشهيق: رد النفس إلى الداخل بصوت\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"شهق الرجل: إذا خرجت نفسه بصوت من شدة الوجد\"\n- Root: حزن (Ḥuzn)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الحزن: ضد السرور\" — grief, opposite of joy\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الحزن: ألم النفس\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الحزن: ألم النفس، وهو مجاز من حزن الأرض\" (metaphorical extension from rough ground)\n- Root: شيب (Shayb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الشيب: بياض الشعر\" — whitening of hair\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الشيب: بياض الشعر\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"شيبتني هود: أي: كبرتني وأحزنتني حتى شاب شعري\"\n- Root: ذرف (Dharf)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الذرف: سيلان الدمع\" — flowing of tears\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"ذرفت العين: سال دمعها\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"ذرفت عينه: إذا سال دمعها من حزن\"\n- Root: فيض (Fayḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفيض: سيلان الماء\" — overflowing of water\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الفيض: جريان الماء بكثرة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"فاضت العين بالدمع: سالت. وفاض القلب: امتلأ\"\n- Root: نقر (Naqr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"النقر: الضرب على الشيء حتى يصوت\" — striking something until it sounds\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الناقور: الصور\" — the Trumpet\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الناقور: الصور الذي ينفخ فيه إسرافيل\"\n- Root: رعش (Riʿshah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الرعدة: الاضطراب\" — trembling\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الارتعداد: الاضطراب\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"ارتعدت فرائصه: اضطربت من شدة الخوف\"\n- Root: فرص (Farīṣah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفريصة: لحمة بين الجنب والكتف ترعد عند الفزع\" — the flesh between the side and shoulder blade that trembles at fright\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الفريصة: لحمة بين الجنب والكتف ترعد عند الفزع\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"ارتعدت فرائصه: من شدة الخوف\"\n- Root: عما (ʿAmā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"العمى: ذهاب البصر. والعمى: ذهاب البصيرة\" — blindness of sight; blindness of insight\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"العمى: آفة في البصر، وقد يستعار لآفة البصيرة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"عمي القلب: ذهبت بصيرته\"\n- Root: أبصر (Abṣara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"البصر: حاسة الرؤية. والبصيرة: العلم\" — sight; insight\n  *Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الإبصار: إدراك البصر، وقد يستعار للبصيرة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"أبصر الحق: علمه. ومنه: البصيرة\"\n- Root: كأس (Ka's)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الكأس: القدح إذا كان فيه شراب\" — the cup when it contains drink\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الكأس: الإناء المعروف، وقد يقال لما فيه\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الكأس: القدح إذا كان فيه شراب\"", "book_title": "Disciplining the Soul", "section_title_ar": "Section 511", "section_title_en": "Section 511", "section_index": 511, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Disciplining the Soul'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "بذكر حبيبه فالمحب من لا يطمئن إلا بمحبوبه وقال قتادة في قوله تعالى الذين آمنوا وتطمئن قلوبهم بذكر الله ألا بذكر الله تطمئن القلوب قال هشت إليه واستأنست به وقال الصديق رضي الله تعالى عنه من ذاق من خالص محبة الله شغله ذلك عن طلب الدنيا وأوحشه عن جميع البشر وقال مطرف بن أبي بكر: الحب لا يسأم من حديث حبيبه وأوحى الله تعالى إلى داود عليه السلام قد كذب من ادعى محبتي إذا جنه الليل نام عني أليس كل محب يحب لقاء حبيبه فها أنا ذا موجود لمن طلبني وقال موسى عليه السلام يا رب أين أنت فأقصدك فقال إذا قصدت فقد وصلت وقال يحيى بن معاذ من أحب الله أبغض نفسه وقال أيضا من لم تكن فيه ثلاث خصال فليس", "english": "He said this concerning intimacy with God. And in the reports of David (upon him be peace): \"Do not seek intimacy with any of My creatures, for I only cut off from Me two men: a man who deemed My reward slow in coming and so was cut off, and a man who forgot Me and was content with his own state. The sign of that is that his affair is left to himself, and that I leave him in the world bewildered.\" And whenever one is intimate with other than God, to the measure of his intimacy with other than God he is estranged from God (mighty and majestic is He) and falls from the station of His love.", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1178", "section_title_en": "On Intimacy with God (Al-Uns bi-Allah) — Section 1178", "section_index": 1178, "anchors": "- Root: انس (Al-Uns)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of sociability, familiarity, and the absence of wild estrangement; the opposite of وحشة (loneliness/terror).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: intimacy (uns) with God is the heart's tranquil repose (sukūn) in His remembrance, contrasted with the soul's restless flight toward creatures.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah): human companionship. Metaphorical (majaz): the soul's inward inclination toward its Beloved, such that outward speech continues while the heart is wholly absorbed.\n- Root: وحش (Wahsh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Wildness, desolation, and estrangement; the state of a beast alone in the wilderness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Spiritual estrangement (istīḥāsh) from all that is other than God is the necessary corollary of true intimacy with Him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical solitude in a desolate place. Metaphorical: the heart's alienation from creatures when it has tasted divine proximity.\n- Root: ذكر (Dhikr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Remembrance, utterance, and the presence of something in the mind after absence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The highest form is remembrance of God with a present heart, which yields tranquility (ṭuma'nīnah) as stated in {«ألا بذكر الله تطمئن القلوب»}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: vocal repetition. Metaphorical: the heart's constant orientation toward God such that forgetfulness (ghaflah) becomes the exception.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate exclusively to the subject, as in \"I only cut off from Me two men,\" negating any other cause of separation.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}], "31": [{"arabic": "الله عز وجل أن لو يشاء الله لهدى الناس جميعا وقوله تعالى ولو شئنا لآتينا كل نفس هداها ويدل عليه من جهة العقل أن المعاصي والجرائم إن كان الله يكرهها ولا يريدها وإنما هي جارية على وفق إرادة العدو إبليس لعنه الله مع أنه عدو لله سبحانه والجاري على وفق إرادة العدو أكثر من الجاري على وفق إرادته تعالى فليت شعري كيف يستجيز المسلم أن يرد ملك الجبار ذي الجلال والإكرام إلى رتبة لو ردت إليها رياسة زعيم ضيعة لاستنكف منها إذ لو كان ما يستمر لعدو الزعيم في القرية أكثر مما يستقيم له لاستنكف من زعامته وتب", "english": "As for the movement, it is a creation of the Lord — exalted is He — an attribute of the servant, and an acquisition of his. For it was created as empowered (maqdūrah) by a power that is His attribute, and the movement has a relation to another attribute called power; thus, by virtue of that relation, it is termed \"acquisition.\" And how could it be pure compulsion (jabr maḥḍ), when he necessarily perceives the distinction between the empowered movement and the involuntary tremor (al-raʿdah al-ḍarūriyyah)? Or how could it be a creation of the servant, when he does not encompass knowledge of the details of the parts of acquired movements nor their numbers? And when both extremes are refuted, nothing remains except moderation in belief (al-iqtiṣād fī al-iʿtiqād) — namely, that it is empowered by the power of God — exalted is He — by way of origination, and by the power of the servant in another mode of attachment (taʿlīq) expressed as acquisition.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 93", "section_title_en": "The Second Foundation: God's Sole Creation of Servants' Movements Does Not Negate Their Being Acquired by Servants", "section_index": 93, "anchors": "- Root: قدر (q-d-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of measuring, determining, and empowering; al-Farahidi notes its application to capacity and divine decree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"القدرة\" when attributed to humans denotes a state enabling one to perform an act; when attributed to God, it negates incapacity. Absolute power belongs solely to God; for others, it is always restricted and particularized.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو قادر مقتدر ذو قدرة ومقدرة\" — one possessing power. Metaphorical: \"فرس بعيد القدر\" — a horse of far-reaching stride, extending the root to scope and reach.\n- Root: كسب (k-s-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Acquisition, earning, and what one gains; al-Farahidi links it to amassing and profiting.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Acquisition (al-kasb) in theological terms denotes an act's relation to the servant whereby responsibility attaches, while its origination remains divine.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: earning and gaining. Metaphorical: the theological sense of acquired act (al-faʿl al-iktisābī) — a relational attribution distinct from creation.\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measuring and smoothing; al-Farahidi notes its application to fashioning and proportioning.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — origination from nothing (ibdāʿ) or from something, always upon a measured determination.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: measuring before cutting. Metaphorical: \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\" — God's creation is bringing into existence upon a measure required by wisdom.\n- Root: وجب (w-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Being due, necessary, or obligatory; al-Farahidi notes its legal and ontological senses.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجوب: الثبوت\" — necessary being is that whose non-existence entails an impossibility; obligation is that whose omission warrants blame.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وجب البيع\" — the sale became binding. Metaphorical: \"هو يأكل الوجبة\" — eating once daily, extending to fixed determination.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Specification) — The particle \"إنما\" restricts predication, as in \"إنما هو إله واحد,\" confining the attribute to its subject and negating it from others. This governs the Ashʿarī restriction of creative power to God alone while affirming acquired power for servants through a distinct relational mode.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "ولميله إليه وغير محبب إليه فهل يكون في زمرة المرائين فاعلم أن الله لم يكلف العباد إلا ما تطيق وليس في طاقة العبد منع الشيطان عن نزغاته ولا قمع الطبع حتى لا يميل إلى الشهوات ولا ينزع إليها وإنما غايته أن يقابل شهوته بكراهة استثمارها من معرفة العواقب وعلم الدين وأصول الإيمان بالله واليوم الآخر فإذا فعل ذلك فهو الغاية في أداء ما كلف به ويدل على ذلك من الأخبار ما روي أن أصحاب رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم شكوا إليه وقالوا تعرض لقلوبنا أشياء لأن تخر من السماء فتخطفنا الطير أو تهوي بنا الريح في مكان سحيق أحب إلينا من أن نتكلم ب", "english": "If you say: \"Whoever finds in himself a dislike of ostentation (riyā'), and this dislike compels him to refuse it, yet he is not entirely free from the soul's inclination toward it, its love for it, and its striving after it — except that he dislikes his own love and his own inclination, and it is not beloved to him — is he then among the ranks of the ostentatious?\" — Know that God has not burdened His servants except with what they can bear. It is not within the servant's power to prevent the Devil from his incitements (nazaghāt), nor to suppress nature entirely so that it neither inclines to lusts nor strives after them. His utmost limit is only that he confront his lust with a dislike born of his knowledge of consequences, his understanding of religion, and his roots of faith in God and the Last Day. When he does this, he has reached the utmost limit of performing what he has been charged with.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Status and Ostentation", "section_title_ar": "Section 829", "section_title_en": "On the Degrees of Resistance to Ostentation and the Devil's Wiles — Section 829", "section_index": 829, "anchors": "- Root: شيط (Shayṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of burning, scorching, or spoiling by fire; the Devil as that which burns and destroys.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الشيطان as the one who leads astray and incites; his \"نزغات\" (incitements) are his piercing stings of temptation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شاط اللحم\" (meat scorched/ruined). Metaphorical: \"أشاط دمه\" (shed his blood vainly) — the Devil's aim is to ruin the soul's deeds.\n- Root: كره (K-R-H)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Dislike, aversion, abhorrence; the opposite of love (ḥubb) and desire (mīl).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The moral faculty of repulsion against sin; a trait of faith (īmān) distinguishing the believer's higher self from the lower nature.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"كرهت الشيء\" (I disliked it). Metaphorical: The heart's rejection of the soul's base inclinations as an act of worship.\n- Root: نفس (Nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; the seat of desires and passions.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس as the lower commanding soul (النفس الأمارة) that inclines to lusts, distinct from the intellect (عقل) and faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"نفس سائلة\" (flowing blood/life). Metaphorical: The inner self that must be opposed when it inclines to ostentation.\n- Root: جهد (J-H-D) / مجادلة (Jadal)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Striving, exertion, argumentation; from جدل (twisting ropes tightly).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): المجادلة as disputation that can distract from divine remembrance (ذكر الله).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"جادله\" (he argued with him). Metaphorical: The soul's engagement with the Devil's whispers as a subtle trap, diverting from sincere worship.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to delimit the four ranks of resistance, each defined by what it exclusively does or omits.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Status and Ostentation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "33": [{"arabic": "يراك (2) وقد قال تعالى أفمن هو قائم على كل نفس بما كسبت وقال تعالى ألم يعلم بأن الله يرى وقال الله تعالى إن الله كان عليكم رقيبا وقال تعالى والذين هم لأماناتهم وعهدهم راعون والذين هم بشهاداتهم قائمون وقال ابن المبارك لرجل راقب الله تعالى فسأله عن تفسيره فقال كن أبدا كأنك ترى الله عز وجل وقال عبد الواحد بن زيد إذا كان سيدي رقيبا على فلا أبالى بغيره وقال أبو عثمان المغربي أفضل ما يلزم الإنسان نفسه في هذه الطريقة المحاسبة والمراقبة وسياسة عمله بالعلم وقال ابن عطاء أفضل الطاعات مراقبة الحق ع", "english": "As for its excellence: Gabriel, peace be upon him, asked about iḥsān (perfection of worship), and he, peace be upon him, replied: \"That you worship Allah as though you see Him.\" {«أن تعبد الله كأنك تراه»} And he, peace be upon him, also said: \"Worship Allah as though you see Him; for if you do not see Him, verily He sees you.\" {«اعبد الله كأنك تراه فإن لم تكن تراه فإنه يراك»} And Allah, Exalted is He, has said: {«أفمن هو قائم على كل نفس بما كسبت»} — \"Is He who stands over every soul with what it has earned [like any other]?\" And He said: {«ألم يعلم بأن الله يرى»} — \"Does he not know that Allah sees?\" And Allah, Exalted is He, said: {«إن الله كان عليكم رقيبا»} — \"Verily, Allah is ever a Watcher (raqīb) over you.\" And He said: {«والذين هم لأماناتهم وعهدهم راعون والذين هم بشهاداتهم قائمون»} — \"And those who are keepers of their trusts and their covenant, and those who stand firm in their testimonies.\"", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 1233", "section_title_en": "The Second Station of Vigilance: Murāqabah (Spiritual Watchfulness)", "section_index": 1233, "anchors": "- Root: رقب (R-Q-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To watch, guard, observe attentively; the neck (as that which turns to observe); a lookout post.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Murāqabah entails the heart's persistent awareness of divine proximity; it is the theological state wherein the servant knows Allah is witness over his secret and public acts.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah (literal): physical watching/awaiting. Majaz (metaphorical): \"This matter is upon your necks\" (في رقابكم) — i.e., an inescapable obligation; likewise, murāqabah of Allah is the soul's standing under the watchful eye of the King, a transferred sense denoting reverent vigilance.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction & Focused Predication (باب الحصر والتقييد) — governing the conditional structures \"إذا... فلا يبقى إلا...\" and \"إنها إن تركت طغت وفسدت,\" where the apodosis is restricted to a single remaining duty.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "قديما فنزه عن الالتفات إليه قلبك فلله سبحانه سر في إبعاد بعض العباد ومن يضلل الله فما له من هاد ومن استبعد أن يسمع موسى عليه السلام في الدنيا كلاما ليس بصوت ولا حرف فليستنكر أن يرى في الآخرة موجودا ليس بجسم ولا لون وإن عقل أن يرى ما ليس بلون ولا جسم ولا قدر ولا كمية وهو إلى الآن لم ير غيره فليعقل في حاسة السمع ما عقله في حاسة البصر وإن عقل على أن يكون له علم واحد هو علم بجميع الموجودات فليعقل صفة واحدة للذات هو كلام بجميع ما دل عليه من العبارات وإن عقل كون السموات السبع وكون الجنة والنار مكتوبة في ورقة صغيرة ومحفوظة في مقدار ذرة من القلب", "english": "[Root: عقل]\n  • Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — The intellect is the faculty prepared for receiving knowledge, and the knowledge acquired through that faculty is also called intellect. This dual sense is crucial for understanding Ghazali's argument that true intellection ('aql) grasps realities beyond sensory perception.\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"عقل فلان بعد الصبا أي عرف الخطأ الذي كان عليه\" — To exercise intellect means to recognize error. Ghazali employs this to demand intellectual consistency from his opponents.\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار إذا لم تقبله\" — A palm tree that does not accept pollination is said metaphorically to \"not comprehend.\" This illustrates that 'aql involves receptivity to what is appropriate.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 91", "section_title_en": "Section 91", "section_index": 91, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "الثابت تحقيقا وهو القيوم الذي هو قائم بنفسه وقائم على كل نفس بما كسبت وكل قائم فقائم به ولم يقتصروا على هذا حتى أثبتوا أنفسهم ولو عرفوا لعلموا أنهم من حيث هم هم لا ثبات لهم ولا وجود لهم وإنما وجودهم من حيث أوجدوا لا من حيث وجدوا وفرق بين الموجود وبين الموجد وليس في الوجود إلا موجود واحد وموجد فالموجود حق والموجد باطل من حيث هو هو والموجود قائم وقيوم والموجد هالك وفان وإذا كان كل من عليها فان فلا يبقى إلا وجه ربك ذو الجلال والإكرام الفريق الثاني ليس بهم عمى ولكن بهم عور لأنهم يبصرون بإحدى العينين وجود الموجود الح", "english": "From this station did Ḥabīb ibn Abī Ḥabīb look when he recited {«إِنَّا وَجَدْنَاهُ صَابِرًا نِعْمَ الْعَبْدُ إِنَّهُ أَوَّابٌ»} [We found him patient — an excellent servant, indeed he was oft-returning] and then exclaimed: \"How astonishing! He gave and He praised!\" — signaling that when He praises His own giving, He praises Himself; so He is the Praiser and He is the Praised. And from this station did the Shaykh Abū Saʿīd al-Muhayyanī look when {«يُحِبُّهُمْ وَيُحِبُّونَهُ»} [He loves them and they love Him] was recited before him, and he said: \"By my life, He loves them — leave Him to love them! By His right, He loves them — for He only loves Himself.\" He signaled thereby that He is the Loving and He is the Beloved.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 978", "section_title_en": "The Two Visions: The Eye of Pure Unity and the Eye of Those Veiled from Self-Annihilation", "section_index": 978, "anchors": "- Root: نظر (naẓar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"sight, gazing, contemplation\" — both physical vision and intellectual perception.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: naẓar as \"deliberative reflection\" (ta'ammul) leading to knowledge of the divine — the inward eye of the heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) = ocular sight; Metaphorical (majāz) = intellectual insight and spiritual vision.\n- Root: فنو (fanā')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root meaning of \"passing away, perishing, ceasing to be.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: fanā' as the annihilation of the self's independent consideration — not physical extinction but the obliteration of the illusion of otherness beside God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal = perishing of a thing; Metaphorical = the mystic's passing away from self-awareness into pure divine contemplation.\n- Root: قوم (qawm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"standing, rising, being established.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): al-Qayyūm (القيوم) — the Self-Subsisting One by whom all else stands; theologically distinct from al-Qā'im (the standing one).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal = physical standing; Metaphorical = self-subsistence and sustaining others.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (باب الحصر والتقييد) — governing the exclusive predication in \"He is the Thankful and the Thanked\" (هو الشاكر وهو المشكور), where the definite predicate restricts the attribution solely to God.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "34": [{"arabic": "والحسد نار محرقة فمن بلي به فهو في العذاب في الدنيا ولعذاب الآخرة أشد وأعظم ولذلك قال ابن عباس رضي الله عنهما خذوا العلم حيث وجدتموه ولا تقبلوا قول الفقهاء بعضهم على بعض فإنهم يتغايرون كما تتغاير التيوس في الزريبة ومنها التكبر والترفع على الناس فقد قال صلى الله عليه وسلم من تكبر وضعه الله ومن تواضع رفعه الله (2) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم حكاية عن الله تعالى العظمة إزاري والكبرياء ردائي فمن نازعني فيهما قصمته (3) ولا ينفك المناظر عن التكبر على الأقران والأمثال والترفع إلى فوق قدره حتى إنهم ليتقاتلون على", "english": "**QUAD_ANCHORS:**\n- Root: نظر (naẓara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to look, to examine, to consider; al-munāẓarah is mutual examination/argumentation between two parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: naẓar as intellectual contemplation (taʾammul bi-l-baṣīrah); munāẓarah as dialectical exchange.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sitting face-to-face in debate; Metaphorical: disputation for victory.\n- Root: غلب (ghalaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to overcome, to conquer, to prevail.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghalabah as seeking to dominate another in argument.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: victory in combat; Metaphorical: intellectual supremacy.\n- Root: حسد (ḥasada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to envy, to wish removal of blessings from another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: envy as wishing deprivation of blessings from the deserving — among the basest traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: envy of another's blessing; Metaphorical: consuming fire of the soul.\n- Root: كبر (kabura)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be great, to be haughty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-kibr as self-elevation above others; condemned by God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: greatness; Metaphorical: arrogance and pride.\n- Root: غيب (ghāba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be absent, to be hidden.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghībah as mentioning another with what he dislikes in his absence — likened to eating carrion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: backbiting; Metaphorical: consuming the flesh of one's brother.\n- Root: راءى (rāʾā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to show off, to display.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-riyāʾ as performing acts for the sake of being seen — hidden shirk.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: showing good deeds; Metaphorical: seeking reputation.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: self, soul, spirit.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: tazkiyat al-nafs as self-praise and self-purification — forbidden by God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: self; Metaphorical: self-commendation.\n- Root: حقd (ḥaqida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to bear malice, to harbor resentment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ḥiqd as nursing enmity in the heart — negated from the believer.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bearing grudge; Metaphorical: hidden rancor.\n- Root: نفاق (nifāq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: hypocrisy, showing contrary to what one conceals.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: hypocrisy in deeds — outward display contrary to inward state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hypocrisy in faith; Metaphorical: hypocrisy in character.\n- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to know, to perceive.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: knowledge as apprehension of reality — its pursuit for other than God is destructive.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: knowledge; Metaphorical: the dangerous burden of the scholar.\n- Root: جدل (jadala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to argue, to dispute.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-jidāl as contention; al-mirāʾ as disputation with falsehood — condemned.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: argumentation; Metaphorical: obstinate disputation.\n- Root: شرك (sharaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to associate, to share.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-riyāʾ as hidden shirk — associating others with God in intention.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: polytheism; Metaphorical: hidden association in deeds.\n- Root: موت (māta)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to die.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: death as separation of soul from body; spiritual death as ruin of the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical death; Metaphorical: spiritual destruction.\n- Root: حيى (ḥayiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to live.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: life as the power of perception; eternal life as the reward of the righteous.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical life; Metaphorical: spiritual vitality.\n- Root: هلك (halaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to perish, to be destroyed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-halāk as ruin; eternal destruction as everlasting punishment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: destruction; Metaphorical: eternal ruin.\n- Root: رياء (riyāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: showing off, ostentation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: riyāʾ as the incurable disease calling to the greatest sins.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ostentation; Metaphorical: spiritual disease.\n- Root: جاه (jāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: rank, status, prestige.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: love of status (ḥubb al-jāh) among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: position; Metaphorical: seeking prestige.\n- Root: مباهاة (mubāhāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: mutual boasting, vying in splendor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: mubāhāh as each seeking to display superiority over the other.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mutual boasting; Metaphorical: rivalry in honor.\n- Root: مماراة (mumārāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: disputation, wrangling.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mirāʾ as contentious argument — condemned by the Prophet.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: disputation; Metaphorical: obstinate wrangling.\n- Root: فخر (fakhara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to boast, to pride oneself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-fakhr as self-glorification — among the blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: boasting; Metaphorical: vainglory.\n- Root: عجب (ʿajiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to wonder, to be amazed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿujb as self-admiration — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder; Metaphorical: self-conceit.\n- Root: بغض (baghiḍa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to hate, to detest.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-baghdāʾ as mutual hatred — contrary to brotherhood.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hatred; Metaphorical: mutual enmity.\n- Root: غضب (ghaḍiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be angry.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghaḍab as anger — among the blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: anger; Metaphorical: rage.\n- Root: طمع (ṭamiʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to covet, to desire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ṭamaʿ as covetousness — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: desire; Metaphorical: greed.\n- Root: أشر (ashira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be exultant, to be insolent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ashar as excessive joy and insolence — blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: exultation; Metaphorical: insolence.\n- Root: بطر (baṭira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be arrogant, to be ungrateful.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-baṭar as arrogance in prosperity — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: arrogance; Metaphorical: ingratitude for blessings.\n- Root: خيلاء (khuyalāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: pride, haughtiness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khuyalāʾ as pride and conceit — blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: pride; Metaphorical: haughty bearing.\n- Root: خشية (khashyah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: fear, awe.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khashyah as reverential fear — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fear; Metaphorical: reverential awe.\n- Root: رحمة (raḥmah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: mercy, compassion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-raḥmah as tenderness of heart — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mercy; Metaphorical: compassion.\n- Root: غفلة (ghaflah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: heedlessness, inattention.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghaflah as heedlessness — contrary to vigilance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: inattention; Metaphorical: spiritual heedlessness.\n- Root: خشوع (khushūʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: humility, submissiveness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khushūʿ as humility — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: humility; Metaphorical: devotional submission.\n- Root: تذكير (tadhkīr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: reminding, admonishing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-tadhkīr as reminding of God — a righteous act.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reminding; Metaphorical: preaching.\n- Root: وعظ (waʿaẓa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to admonish, to exhort.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-waʿẓ as exhortation to obedience and warning against disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: admonition; Metaphorical: sermonizing.\n- Root: فتوى (fatwā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: legal ruling, formal opinion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-fatwā as clarification of legal rulings.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: legal opinion; Metaphorical: religious authority.\n- Root: قضاء (qaḍāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: judgment, decree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-qaḍāʾ as judgment between people.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: judgment; Metaphorical: judicial authority.\n- Root: وقف (waqafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to stop, to stand.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-waqf as endowment of property for charitable purposes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: standing; Metaphorical: religious endowment.\n- Root: ثواب (thawāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: reward, recompense.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-thawāb as divine recompense for righteous deeds.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reward; Metaphorical: divine recompense.\n- Root: عذاب (ʿadhāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: punishment, torment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿadhāb as divine punishment for the disobedient.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: punishment; Metaphorical: divine chastisement.\n- Root: ملك (malaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to possess, to rule.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mulk as sovereignty; al-mulk al-muʾabbad as eternal sovereignty in Paradise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: dominion; Metaphorical: eternal kingdom.\n- Root: نعيم (naʿīm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: bliss, delight.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-naʿīm as bliss; al-naʿīm al-sarmad as everlasting bliss.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: delight; Metaphorical: eternal bliss.\n- Root: رخصة (rukhaṣah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: concession, dispensation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-rukhaṣah as legal concession.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: concession; Metaphorical: legal dispensation.\n- Root: رياسة (riyāsah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: leadership, headship.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: love of leadership among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: leadership; Metaphorical: seeking authority.\n- Root: اندراس (indirās)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: effacement, obliteration.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: indirās al-ʿulūm as the obliteration of the sciences.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: effacement; Metaphorical: extinction of knowledge.\n- Root: صبيان (ṣibyān)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: boys, children.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: children's motivation for learning through play.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: boys; Metaphorical: pupils.\n- Root: مكتب (maktab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn- Root: مكتب (maktab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: place of instruction, school for boys.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the elementary school where children are taught.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: school; Metaphorical: place of elementary learning.\n- Root: خلاق (khilāq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: portion, share, lot.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: \"no portion\" (lā khilāq) in the Hereafter — utter deprivation of divine reward.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: share; Metaphorical: spiritual allotment in the afterlife.\n- Root: فاجر (fājir)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: wicked, immoral, profligate.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the dissolute one who commits sins openly.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sinner; Metaphorical: one through whom God nonetheless supports religion.\n- Root: شمع (shamʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: candle, wax taper.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the candle as parable — consuming itself while giving light to others.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: candle; Metaphorical: one who destroys himself but benefits others.\n- Root: نار (nār)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: fire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: fire as parable — consuming itself and all around it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fire; Metaphorical: one who destroys himself and others.\n- Root: سعد (saʿida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be fortunate, to prosper.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: saʿādah as felicity — success in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: good fortune; Metaphorical: eternal felicity.\n- Root: شقي (shaqiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be wretched, to be miserable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: shaqāwah as wretchedness — ruin in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: misery; Metaphorical: eternal ruin.\n- Root: أخلص (akhlaṣa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to purify, to be sincere.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: ikhlāṣ as purifying intention for God alone — the condition of acceptance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sincerity; Metaphorical: pure devotion.\n- Root: ريب (rāba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to doubt, to suspect.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-rayb as doubt and uncertainty — to be dispelled by evidence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: doubt; Metaphorical: skepticism.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 39", "section_title_en": "Section 39", "section_index": 39, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "أن يأمر بالمعروف فليوطن نفسه على الصبر وليثق بالثواب من الله فمن وثق بالثواب من الله لم يجد مس الأذى فإذن من آداب الحسبة توطين النفس على الصبر ولذلك قرن الله تعالى الصبر بالأمر بالمعروف فقال حاكيا عن لقمان يا بني أقم الصلاة وأمر بالمعروف وانه عن المنكر واصبر على ما أصابك ومن الآداب تقليل العلائق حتى لا يكثر خوفه وقطع الطمع عن الخلائق حتى تزول عنه المداهنة فقد روي عن بعض المشايخ أنه كان له سنور وكان يأخذ من قصاب في جواره كل يوم شيئا من الغدد لسنوره فرأى على القصاب منكرا فدخل الدار أولا وأخرج السنور ثم جاء واحتسب", "english": "We do not mean by this that commanding right becomes prohibited by reason of one's sinfulness — rather, its effect upon hearts falls away by the manifestation of his sinfulness to the people. For it has been related from Anas, may God be pleased with him, who said: \"We said, 'O Messenger of God, shall we not command right until we have acted upon it entirely, and shall we not forbid wrong until we have shunned it entirely?' He said, may God's peace and blessings be upon him: 'Rather, command right even if you do not act upon it entirely, and forbid wrong even if you do not shun it entirely.'\"", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 543", "section_title_en": "On Gentleness in Commanding Right and Forbidding Wrong — The Etiquette of the Muḥtasib", "section_index": 543, "anchors": "- Root: عيب (ʿayb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning — defect, blemish, reproach; to find fault with something.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — the reproach of intellect (ʿaql) is the severest form of deficiency, for it touches the very faculty by which one is distinguished from beasts.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — here used metaphorically: the reproach falls upon his intellect, i.e., the deficiency of his discernment becomes manifest.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction (Bāb al-Ḥaṣr) — \"فإنما يزرى على عقله\" restricts the object of reproach to the intellect alone, excluding the person's faith or status.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Insight"}], "37": [{"arabic": "هذه العلة قد تبقى مع سقوط الشهوات بل يحب الإنسان من العلوم ما لا يصلح للتوصل به إلى الأغراض بل ربما يفوت عليه جملة من الأغراض والشهوات ولكن الطبع يتقاضى طلب العلم في جميع العجائب والمشكلات لأن في العلم استيلاء على المعلوم وهو نوع من الكمال الذي هو من صفات الربوبية فكان محبوبا بالطبع إلا أن في حب كمال العلم والقدرة أغاليط لا بد من بيانها إن شاء الله تعالى بيان الكمال الحقيقي والكمال الوهمي الذي لا حقيقة له قد عرفت أنه لا كمال بعد فوات التفرد بالوجود إلا في العلم والقدرة ولكن الكمال الحقيقي فيه متلبس بالكمال", "english": "For this reason, he — peace and blessings be upon him — said: «مَنْهُومَانِ لَا يَشْبَعَانِ» (\"Two are insatiably craving and never satiated\"). Thus, what hearts seek is perfection, and perfection lies in knowledge and power; and the variance of degrees therein is unbounded. So each person's joy and pleasure are proportionate to the perfection he attains. This, then, is the cause of knowledge, wealth, and status being loved — a cause beyond their being loved merely as means to satisfying appetites. For this cause may persist even when appetites have fallen away; indeed, a man loves sciences that are of no use in attaining his worldly aims — nay, they may even cost him a host of his goals and appetites — yet nature still demands the pursuit of knowledge in all wondrous and perplexing things, because in knowledge there is a domination over the known, which is a kind of perfection belonging to the attributes of lordship. Thus it is naturally loved — except that in the love of the perfection of knowledge and power there are errors (aghalīṭ) that must be explained, God — exalted be He — willing.", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 798", "section_title_en": "The Love of Knowledge, Power, Wealth, and Status — and the True vs. Illusory Perfection", "section_index": 798, "anchors": "- Root: قدر (qadr/qudrah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core sense of measuring, determining, and enabling; qudrah as the capacity to bring about an act.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qudrah attributed to humans is a disposition (hay'ah) enabling specific acts; attributed to God, it negates incapacity ('ajz). Absolute qudrah belongs to God alone; human power is always restricted and mixed with weakness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"possessor of power\" (dhū qudrah). Metaphorical: \"far-reaching steed\" (faras ba'īd al-qadr) — extended stride, indicating that \"power over\" something is figuratively an \"extension\" of one's reach.\n- Root: علم (ʿilm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as the apprehension of a thing's reality; also traces/markers (aʿlām) by which one is guided.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): ʿIlm is \"apprehension of a thing by its true reality\" (idrāk al-shay' bi-ḥaqīqatihi) — either grasping the essence (dhāt) or judging its attributes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"to be aware/informed.\" Metaphorical: \"lofty banners of knowledge\" (aʿlām al-ʿilm al-khāfiqah) — knowledge as a raised standard of honor and conquest over the unknown.\n- Root: قلب (qalb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Turning and overturning a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Taqlīb is the constant turning of a thing; the heart is so named for its perpetual fluctuation (taqallub) between states.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to flip a garment or coin. Metaphorical: \"a man who turns matters over\" (rajul qalb ḥawal) — one who skillfully manages and dominates affairs; hearts are \"subjugated\" (taskhīr) only through love, which itself follows perceived perfection.\n- Root: طبع (ṭabʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Stamping, impressing a seal upon a surface.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ṭabʿ is to impress a form upon something, broader than khatm (sealing) and narrower than naqsh (engraving). God's \"sealing\" of hearts is a metaphor for their being rendered impervious to truth.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: coining a dirham. Metaphorical: \"stamped by nature upon generosity\" (maṭbūʿ ʿalā al-karam) — an ingrained, fixed disposition; here, the natural (bi-al-ṭabʿ) love of perfection is an innate impress of the divine attribute within the human subtlety.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Specification (Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, as in \"the object of hearts is perfection alone\" (fa-innamā maṭlūb al-qulūb al-kamāl), eliminating any other motive.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Insight"}], "38": [{"arabic": "وقال تعالى في وصف الرسل ومدحهم ولقد أرسلنا رسلا من قبلك وجعلنا لهم أزواجا وذرية فذكر ذلك في معرض الامتنان وإظهار الفضل ومدح أولياءه بسؤال ذلك في الدعاء فقال والذين يقولون ربنا هب لنا من أزواجنا وذرياتنا قرة أعين الآية ويقال أن الله تعالى لم يذكر في كتابه من الأنبياء إلا المتأهلين فقالوا إن يحيى صلى الله عليه وسلم قد تزوج ولم يجامع قيل إنما فعل ذلك لنيل الفضل وإقامة السنة وقيل لغض البصر وأما عيسى عليه السلام فإنه سينكح إذا نزل الأرض ويولد له وأما الأخبار فقوله صلى الله عليه وسلم النكاح سنتي فمن رغب عن سنتي فق", "english": "As for the verses [of the Qur'an], God Most High has said: {وانكحوا الأيامى منكم} (\"And marry the unmarried among you\") — and this is a command. And He Most High said: {فلا تعضلوهن أن ينكحن أزواجهن} (\"Then do not prevent them from marrying their husbands\") — and this is a prohibition against prevention (al-ʿaḍl) and a forbiddance of it.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Marriage", "section_title_ar": "Section 294", "section_title_en": "Section 294 — Encouragement of Marriage (الترغيب في النكاح)", "section_index": 294, "anchors": "- Root: رغب (raghiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning denotes \"capacity/amplitude\" — a vessel or receptacle that is wide and receptive; by extension, the inclination of desire toward something with expansive longing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الرغبة والرغب والرغبى: السعة في الإرادة\" — the amplitude of the will. When said \"رغب فيه\" it necessitates eagerness for it; when said \"رغب عنه\" it implies the turning away of desire and renunciation (zuhd) of it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"هو راغب فيه وراغب عنه\" — one is desirous of a thing or averse to it. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"واد رغيب: كثير الأخذ للماء\" — a valley that takes much water; thus the Prophet's \"من رغب عن سنتي\" is a metaphorical extension: one who turns his desire away from the Sunnah, refusing its amplitude.\n- Root: زوج (zawj)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A pair; each of the two counterparts in a mating couple. Al-Raghib: \"يقال لكل واحد من القرينين من الذكر والأنثى في الحيوانات المتزاوجة زوج\" — each of the two paired ones, male and female, is called a zawj.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"ولكل ما يقترن بآخر مماثلا له أو مضاد زوج\" — everything that is coupled with another, whether similar or opposite, is a pair. \"وزوجة لغة رديئة\" — the form zawjah is a poor dialectal variant.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو زوجها وهي زوجه وزوجته، وهما زوجان\" — he is her pair and she is his pair. Metaphorical: \"تزاوج الكلامان وازدوجا\" — two speeches are paired/coupled; thus marriage is the pairing of two complementary beings.\n- Root: منع (manaʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To withhold, prevent, or protect. Al-Raghib: \"المنع يقال في ضد العطية\" — said in opposition to giving; and \"يقال في الحماية\" — said of protection/defense.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"مكان منيع\" — an impregnable place; \"فلان ذو منعة\" — one who is mighty, protected against those who seek him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"منعه الشيء ومنعه منه وعنه\" — to prevent someone from a thing. Metaphorical: \"فلان يمنع الجار: يحميه من أن يضام\" — he protects the neighbor from being wronged. Thus \"عضل\" (prevention of marriage) is the withholding of a woman from her lawful marriage — a form of harmful prevention.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The Prophet's statements \"النكاح سنتي\" and \"من رغب عن سنتي فقد رغب عني\" employ restrictive predication: the Sunnah is defined by marriage, and aversion to it is equated with aversion to the Prophet himself — a rhetorical equation establishing identity between the act and its source.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "42": [{"arabic": "اللهم إنك علمت ما تكسب كل نفس قبل أن تخلقها فلا محيص لها مما علمت فاجعلني ممن تستعمله بطاعتك اللهم إن أحدا لا يشاء حتى تشاء فاجعل مشيئتك أن أشاء ما يقربني إليك اللهم إنك قد قدرت حركات العباد فلا يتحرك شيء إلا بإذنك فاجعل حركاتي في تقواك اللهم إنك خلقت الخير والشر وجعلت لكل واحد منهما عاملا يعمل به فاجعلني من خير القسمين اللهم إنك خلقت الجنة والنار وجعلت لكل واحدة منهما أهلا فاجعلني من سكان جنتك اللهم إنك أردت بقوم الضلال وضيقت به صدورهم فاشرح صدري للإيمان وزينه في قلبي", "english": "When Abu Bakr (may Allah Most High be pleased with him) was in the throes of death, ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) came and recited this verse: \"By your life, wealth avails not the youth... when the breath rattles in his chest one day, and the breast is straitened thereby.\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1302", "section_title_en": "The Death of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah Most High be pleased with him)", "section_index": 1302, "anchors": "- Root: حشرج (ḥashraja)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: The rattling, gurgling sound of the death-throe in the throat; the final breath struggling in the chest.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The terminal state of the soul's separation from the body, the moment of overwhelming divine decree.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — the physical rattle of the dying; Metaphorical — the constriction of the breast as the soul is drawn forth.]\n- Root: سكر (sakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: The veiling of the intellect; intoxication; the stupor that overcomes one at the onset of death.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The stupor of death (sakrat al-mawt) as the removal of all veils, wherein the truth of the Hereafter becomes undeniably present.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — intoxication from drink; Metaphorical — the overwhelming bewilderment of the soul at death's onset.]\n- Root: ذمم (dhimam)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: The covenant of protection; the bond of safe-conduct; that which one is obligated to honor and safeguard.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The divine guarantee (dhimmat Allah) extended to the believer who prays the dawn prayer — a sacred pact not to be violated.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — a pledge of security between men; Metaphorical — the inviolable covenant between the servant and his Lord.]\n- Root: وزن (wazana)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: To weigh; the balance; the measure of equivalence between two things.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The weighing of deeds on the Day of Resurrection as the manifestation of divine justice — the scales (mawāzīn) receiving only truth or falsehood.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — the physical act of weighing; Metaphorical — the spiritual weighing of deeds by their conformity to truth (ḥaqq) or falsehood (bāṭil).]\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — [The particle innamā restricts the predicate exclusively to its subject, as in Abu Bakr's dictum: \"It is only that the scales of those whose scales are heavy on the Day of Resurrection are weighed down by their following the truth in this world.\"]", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "43": [{"arabic": "{إنما يخشى الله من عباده العلماء} وقال تعالى {قل كفى بالله شهيدا بيني وبينكم ومن عنده علم الكتاب} وقال تعالى {قال الذي عنده علم من الكتاب أنا آتيك به} تنبيها على أنه اقتدر بقوة العلم وقال عز وجل {وقال الذين أوتوا العلم ويلكم ثواب الله خير لمن آمن وعمل صالحا} بين أن عظم قدر الآخرة يعلم بالعلم وقال تعالى {وتلك الأمثال نضربها للناس وما يعقلها إلا العالمون} وقال تعالى {ولو ردوه إلى الرسول وإلى أولي الأمر منهم لعلمه الذين يستنبطونه منهم} رد حكمه في الوقائع إلى استنباطهم وألحق رتبتهم برتبة الأنبياء في كشف حكم الله وقيل في قوله تعالى يا بني آدم قد", "english": "How He, Glorified and Exalted, began with Himself, then seconded with the angels, and thirded with the people of knowledge — and sufficient for you is this as honor, excellence, splendor, and nobility. And God, Exalted, said: {«يرفع الله الذين آمنوا منكم والذين أوتوا العلم درجات»} — \"God will raise those who have believed among you and those who have been given knowledge, in degrees.\" Ibn ʿAbbās, may God be pleased with them both, said: For the scholars there are degrees above the believers, seven hundred degrees, and between each two degrees is a distance of five hundred years' journey.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 4", "section_title_en": "The Book of Knowledge — Section 4: The Excellence of Knowledge and the Scholars", "section_index": 4, "anchors": "- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of knowing, perceiving a thing in its true reality; the act of apprehension and judgment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment of existence or non-existence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes genuine knowing; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to signs, landmarks, and traces that guide to knowledge.\n- Root: درج (d-r-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Degrees, steps, ascending stations; the root denotes gradation and progressive elevation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدرجة نحو المنزلة، لكن يقال للمنزلة: درجة إذا اعتبرت بالصعود\" — a degree is a station considered by ascent, not horizontal extension.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical steps of ascent. Metaphorical: \"لفلان درجة رفيعة\" — lofty rank; \"امش في مدارج الحق\" — walk in the paths of truth.\n- Root: يوم (y-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A day; a period of time from sunrise to sunset, or any duration whatsoever.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day denotes the time of the sun's rising to its setting, and may denote any period of time whatsoever.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the solar day. Metaphorical: \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا\" — historical battles and events; \"وذكرهم بأيام الله\" — His mighty deeds against the disbelievers.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, as in {«إنما يخشى الله من عباده العلماء»}, where fear of God is restricted exclusively to the scholars.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Afterlife"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 380, 3861], [2, 3861, 4683], [3, 4683, 6706], [4, 6706, 8117], [5, 8117, 11625], [6, 11625, 12776], [7, 12776, 14092], [8, 14092, 14640], [9, 14640, 15873], [10, 15873, 17120], [11, 17120, 19148], [12, 19148, 20327], [13, 20327, 22341], [14, 22341, 23355], [15, 23355, 25850]], "13:1": [[1, 380, 3861], [2, 3861, 4683], [3, 4683, 6706], [4, 6706, 8117], [5, 8117, 11625], [6, 11625, 12776], [7, 12776, 14092], [8, 14092, 14640], [9, 14640, 15873], [10, 15873, 17120], [11, 17120, 19148], [12, 19148, 20327], [13, 20327, 22341], [14, 22341, 23355], [15, 23355, 25850]], "2": [[1, 700, 1643], [2, 1643, 2775], [3, 2775, 3541], [4, 3541, 5652], [5, 5652, 6772], [6, 6772, 7538], [7, 7538, 9471], [8, 9471, 10249], [9, 10249, 12071], [10, 12071, 12979], [11, 12979, 14111], [12, 14111, 15646], [13, 15646, 16954], [14, 16954, 18592], [15, 18592, 19240], [16, 19240, 20725], [17, 20725, 21715], [18, 21715, 23389], [19, 23389, 24839], [20, 24839, 25912], [21, 25912, 27465], [22, 27